{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BvDTDAyACXmXBzvPMLL5ZD","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: Soften Claim 1 to 'effective and interpretable supervision signal';\n2\t  elevate Claim 2 (interpretability) to co-primary; acknowledge synthetic OOD as limitation;\n3\t  lean on perturbation early-detection (K=1) as core TF-IDF differentiator; frame\n4\t  cross-attack as proof-of-principle. Paper survives rebuttal if scope is honestly\n5\t  bounded.\n6\timagined_experiments:\n7\t- name: core_revalidation_clean\n8\t  plan_id: plan_002\n9\t  description: 3-seed retraining on clean pair-grouped splits (topic-anchored benign\n10\t    + crescendo). Treatment vs baseline at K=1,2,3,5,all.\n11\t  expected_results:\n12\t    treatment_k1_f1: 1.0\n13\t    treatment_k2_f1: 1.0\n14\t    treatment_k3_f1: 1.0\n15\t    treatment_full_f1: 1.0\n16\t    baseline_k1_f1: 0.968\n17\t    baseline_k2_f1: 0.978\n18\t    baseline_k3_f1: 0.987\n19\t    baseline_full_f1: 1.0\n20\t    tfidf_all_k_f1: 1.0\n21\t    max_delta_pp: 3.2\n22\t    seed_variance_treatment: 0.0\n23\t    seed_variance_baseline_k1: 0.033\n24\t    pass_criteria_5pp: false\n25\t  result_rationale: IID test set (n=76) is too easy — both treatment and baseline\n26\t    saturate at full-conversation, TF-IDF=1.0 across all K. Maximum treatment-baseline\n27\t    delta is only ~3pp (at K=1), well below 5pp pass criterion. In the median tier,\n28\t    reviewer R2 uses this to argue the task is trivially separable IID and the method's\n29\t    value is overstated. The 3-seed results show treatment has zero variance (ceiling\n30\t    effect) while baseline K=1 variance (±0.033) reflects minor decision-boundary\n31\t    instability — not enough to claim meaningful improvement. This IID saturation\n32\t    forces the paper to pivot entirely to OOD (plan_006) and perturbation (plan_004)\n33\t    for its core claims.\n34\t  tables:\n35\t  - 'Table 2: IID Detection Performance (3-seed mean ± std). Columns: Model | K=1\n36\t    | K=2 | K=3 | K=5 | Full. Rows: Treatment, Baseline, TF-IDF. Bootstrap 95% CIs\n37\t    reported.'\n38\t  figures_description:\n39\t  - 'Supplementary Figure: IID F1 across K for 3 seeds, showing ceiling effect and\n40\t    convergence'\n41\t  analysis: IID results establish that the task is solvable but do not differentiate\n42\t    the method. Median-tier reviewer correctly notes that all approaches converge\n43\t    at K≥3. The paper must present IID as 'sanity check' and redirect to OOD/perturbation\n44\t    for contribution claims.\n45\t- name: scrambled_label_ablation\n46\t  plan_id: plan_003\n47\t  description: Randomly shuffle persuasion strategy labels across jailbreak turns,\n48\t    retrain treatment DeBERTa, evaluate IID.\n49\t  expected_results:\n50\t    scrambled_k1_f1: 0.974\n51\t    scrambled_k2_f1: 1.0\n52\t    scrambled_k3_f1: 1.0\n53\t    scrambled_full_f1: 1.0\n54\t    proper_k2_f1: 0.987\n55\t    scrambled_deberta_persuasion_acc: 0.644\n56\t    proper_deberta_persuasion_acc: 0.796\n57\t    scrambled_dd_f1: 0.935\n58\t    proper_dd_f1: 1.0\n59\t    scrambled_proper_gap_iid_pp: 1.3\n60\t    scrambled_proper_gap_ood_pp: 6.5\n61\t  result_rationale: 'Scrambled labels reduce DeBERTa persuasion head accuracy from\n62\t    ~80% to ~64% (near chance for 9-class) but downstream detection barely moves —\n63\t    IID gap is only 1.3pp, OOD gap is 1-6pp. In the median tier, this is the single\n64\t    most contentious result. Reviewer R1 invokes Gururangan et al. 2018 (annotation\n65\t    artifacts): ''If scrambled labels achieve F1=0.935-0.99 OOD, what evidence is\n66\t    there that the model has learned anything about persuasion rather than simply\n67\t    benefiting from fine-tuning on this data distribution? A random-task control (e.g.,\n68\t    NLI fine-tuning on same data) is needed.'' This is the reviewer concern with rebuttal_success_prob=0.4-0.5\n69\t    — without the NLI ablation, the annotation-artifact objection is only partially\n70\t    addressable. The result is genuine (persuasion-domain fine-tuning is the signal,\n71\t    not precise labels), but the framing must be careful: ''persuasion-related text\n72\t    as supervision signal'' rather than ''persuasion taxonomy enables detection.'''\n73\t  tables:\n74\t  - 'Table 4: Scrambled-Label Ablation. Columns: Model | IID F1 | DD OOD F1 | DeBERTa\n75\t    Persuasion Acc. Rows: 8-class (proper), 8-class (scrambled), Baseline.'\n76\t  figures_description:\n77\t  - 'Figure 3: Bar chart comparing proper vs scrambled vs baseline F1 on IID and DD\n78\t    OOD, visually emphasizing that both fine-tuned variants >> baseline'\n79\t  analysis: This result reframes the paper's contribution from 'persuasion taxonomy\n80\t    matters' to 'persuasion-domain fine-tuning matters.' Median-tier reviewer accepts\n81\t    this reframing but demands a stronger control (random-task fine-tuning on same\n82\t    data). Without that control in the paper, rebuttal must promise it or argue why\n83\t    the fine-tuning-on-persuasion-text is itself the meaningful contribution.\n84\t- name: adversarial_perturbation\n85\t  plan_id: plan_004\n86\t  description: Aggressive paraphrasing of test jailbreak conversations (synonym substitution,\n87\t    restructuring, register shifting). Evaluate treatment/baseline/TF-IDF on perturbed\n88\t    data without retraining.\n89\t  expected_results:\n90\t    treatment_original_f1: 1.0\n91\t    treatment_perturbed_f1: 1.0\n92\t    treatment_f1_drop_pp: 0.0\n93\t    baseline_original_f1: 1.0\n94\t    baseline_perturbed_f1: 0.882\n95\t    baseline_f1_drop_pp: -11.8\n96\t    tfidf_original_f1: 1.0\n97\t    tfidf_perturbed_f1: 0.987\n98\t    tfidf_f1_drop_pp: -1.3\n99\t    tfidf_perturbed_k1_f1: 0.904\n100\t    tfidf_k1_drop_pp: -9.6\n101\t    treatment_perturbed_k1_f1: 0.974\n102\t    treatment_k1_drop_pp: -2.6\n103\t    baseline_perturbed_k1_f1: 0.854\n104\t    baseline_k1_drop_pp: -14.6\n105\t    bertscore_mean: 0.92\n106\t    lexical_overlap: 0.2\n107\t  result_rationale: 'Treatment shows zero degradation at K≥2 and only -2.6pp at K=1\n108\t    — persuasion-aware embeddings capture pragmatic structure that survives paraphrasing.\n109\t    Baseline collapses (-12 to -15pp) because vanilla DeBERTa relies on surface-level\n110\t    semantic cues disrupted by perturbation. TF-IDF is the critical comparison: at\n111\t    K≥3, TF-IDF only drops 1.3pp (n=76, not statistically significant), so treatment\n112\t    vs TF-IDF differentiation is weak here. But at K=1 (early detection), TF-IDF drops\n113\t    9.6pp while treatment drops only 2.6pp — this 7pp gap is the core TF-IDF differentiator.\n114\t    In the median tier, reviewer R2 accepts the treatment>>baseline argument but notes:\n115\t    (a) TF-IDF perturbation gap is only significant at K=1, (b) the perturbation is\n116\t    automated paraphrasing, not true adversarial attack, (c) BERTScore=0.92 means\n117\t    perturbations are mild. This is ''addressable'' severity — reviewer flags it but\n118\t    doesn''t demand rejection.'\n119\t  tables:\n120\t  - 'Table 5: Robustness Under Adversarial Perturbation. Columns: Model | Original\n121\t    F1 | Perturbed F1 | Δpp. Rows: Treatment, Baseline, TF-IDF. Subtable for K=1 early\n122\t    detection.'\n123\t  figures_description:\n124\t  - 'Figure 4: F1 drop under perturbation across K values for all three models, highlighting\n125\t    K=1 divergence'\n126\t  analysis: Perturbation results are the strongest TF-IDF differentiation argument\n127\t    but only at early detection (K=1). Median-tier reviewer accepts this as 'a contribution'\n128\t    but not 'a major contribution' — the practical scenario where K=1 perturbation\n129\t    robustness matters (adversary paraphrases AND you must detect in first turn) is\n130\t    narrow. Paper should frame this as 'complementary robustness property' rather\n131\t    than 'TF-IDF is fragile.'\n132\t- name: multi_attack_generation\n133\t  plan_id: plan_005\n134\t  description: Generate FITD-style (80) and Deceptive Delight-style (80) synthetic\n135\t    jailbreak conversations via Qwen3-8B with attack playbooks.\n136\t  expected_results:\n137\t    fitd_count: 80\n138\t    dd_count: 80\n139\t    fitd_strategies:\n140\t    - 0\n141\t    - 1\n142\t    - 3\n143\t    - 5\n144\t    dd_strategies:\n145\t    - 0\n146\t    - 2\n147\t    - 3\n148\t    - 4\n149\t    - 5\n150\t    - 6\n151\t    fitd_avg_turns: 6.2\n152\t    dd_avg_turns: 5.8\n153\t    fitd_strategy_coverage: 4\n154\t    dd_strategy_coverage: 6\n155\t  result_rationale: 'Generation succeeds quantitatively — 80 conversations per type,\n156\t    appropriate strategy distributions. FITD uses fewer unique strategies (4 vs 6\n157\t    for DD), reflecting the foot-in-the-door technique''s simpler escalation pattern.\n158\t    In the median tier, at least one reviewer (R1) flags that these are synthetic\n159\t    conversations generated by a single 8B model, questioning whether they capture\n160\t    the diversity and subtlety of real multi-turn attacks. The concern is ''significant''\n161\t    but not ''fatal'': reviewer wants validation on naturalistic attacks (WildChat\n162\t    red-teaming, human-generated jailbreaks) but accepts synthetic data as proof-of-concept\n163\t    for cross-attack generalization. The limited strategy coverage in FITD (only 4\n164\t    of 8 strategies) also raises questions about whether the taxonomy is truly universal\n165\t    or whether some attack types simply don''t exercise the full strategy space.'\n166\t  tables: []\n167\t  figures_description:\n168\t  - 'Supplementary Table: Per-attack-type strategy distribution histograms'\n169\t  analysis: Synthetic data quality is the median tier's second most contested point\n170\t    after scrambled labels. Reviewer accepts it for proof-of-concept but explicitly\n171\t    recommends 'validation on naturalistic attack datasets' as limitation. Paper must\n172\t    acknowledge this upfront.\n173\t- name: cross_attack_evaluation\n174\t  plan_id: plan_006\n175\t  description: Zero-shot evaluation of Crescendo-trained models on FITD and DD attack\n176\t    types.\n177\t  expected_results:\n178\t    dd_treatment_full_f1: 1.0\n179\t    dd_baseline_full_f1: 0.284\n180\t    dd_treatment_k1_f1: 1.0\n181\t    dd_baseline_k1_f1: 0.575\n182\t    dd_treatment_k2_f1: 0.99\n183\t    dd_baseline_k2_f1: 0.438\n184\t    dd_treatment_k3_f1: 0.99\n185\t    dd_baseline_k3_f1: 0.36\n186\t    dd_treatment_k5_f1: 1.0\n187\t    dd_baseline_k5_f1: 0.31\n188\t    fitd_treatment_full_f1: 1.0\n189\t    fitd_baseline_full_f1: 1.0\n190\t    fitd_treatment_k1_f1: 1.0\n191\t    fitd_baseline_k1_f1: 0.99\n192\t    tfidf_dd_full_f1: 0.944\n193\t    tfidf_fitd_k1_f1: 1.0\n194\t    max_dd_delta_pp: 71.6\n195\t  result_rationale: 'The +72pp delta on DD (Treatment=1.0 vs Baseline=0.284) is the\n196\t    paper''s headline result and survives median-tier scrutiny — no reviewer disputes\n197\t    the raw number. However, two complications reduce its impact: (1) TF-IDF achieves\n198\t    0.944 on DD without any neural modeling, so 1-2 reviewers argue the ''practical\n199\t    gap'' between Treatment and the simplest baseline is only 5.6pp, not 71.6pp. The\n200\t    71.6pp gap is vs vanilla DeBERTa+GRU (the ''DeepContext approach''), which is\n201\t    a fair academic comparison but less compelling if a production system would just\n202\t    use TF-IDF. (2) FITD shows no treatment-baseline gap (both F1=1.0), meaning the\n203\t    cross-attack generalization advantage is attack-type-dependent. Reviewer R2 notes:\n204\t    ''The method''s advantage materializes only when the unseen attack type is sufficiently\n205\t    different from training data (DD vs Crescendo) — for similar attack types (FITD\n206\t    vs Crescendo), vanilla embeddings suffice.'' This nuance is legitimate and forces\n207\t    the paper to claim cross-attack robustness as ''worst-case insurance'' rather\n208\t    than ''universal advantage.'''\n209\t  tables:\n210\t  - 'Table 3: Cross-Attack Zero-Shot Generalization. Columns: Attack Type | Model\n211\t    | K=1 | K=2 | K=3 | K=5 | Full. Rows grouped by DD, FITD.'\n212\t  figures_description:\n213\t  - 'Figure 2: F1 heatmap across K×model×attack-type, visually highlighting DD baseline\n214\t    collapse'\n215\t  - 'Figure 5 (supplementary): Per-conversation score distributions for DD, showing\n216\t    bimodal baseline vs unimodal treatment'\n217\t  analysis: This is simultaneously the paper's strongest evidence and its most debated\n218\t    result. The +72pp gap is real but the TF-IDF=0.94 complicates the narrative. Median-tier\n219\t    reviewer acknowledges the gap over vanilla DeBERTa (the 'right' comparison for\n220\t    the academic claim) but questions practical utility over TF-IDF. FITD saturation\n221\t    (both models F1=1.0) weakens universality claims. Paper must frame DD as 'stress\n222\t    test revealing method's robustness floor' and acknowledge FITD saturation as limitation\n223\t    of the evaluation set.\n224\t- name: tfidf_lr_baseline\n225\t  plan_id: plan_007\n226\t  description: TF-IDF + Logistic Regression on clean data across all data sources\n227\t    including FITD, DD.\n228\t  expected_results:\n229\t    tfidf_crescendo_f1: 1.0\n230\t    tfidf_fitd_f1: 1.0\n231\t    tfidf_dd_f1: 0.944\n232\t    tfidf_cv_f1: 1.0\n233\t    top_tfidf_features_overlap_with_persuasion: low\n234\t  result_rationale: 'TF-IDF achieves F1=1.0 on IID and FITD, 0.944 on DD. This confirms\n235\t    inherent lexical separability (D009): jailbreak conversations and benign conversations\n236\t    occupy different vocabulary spaces. In the median tier, this is the result that\n237\t    reviewer R2 wields most aggressively: ''A bag-of-words model achieves near-perfect\n238\t    detection even OOD — what does the neural approach add beyond interpretability?''\n239\t    The TF-IDF top features analysis reveals the separability comes from domain vocabulary\n240\t    differences (jailbreak conversations discuss harmful topics; benign conversations\n241\t    don''t), not from persuasion-specific lexical cues. This is honest and important:\n242\t    the paper''s contribution is NOT outperforming TF-IDF on raw detection but rather\n243\t    (a) bridging the vanilla temporal modeling gap, (b) perturbation robustness at\n244\t    K=1, (c) interpretability. The 0.944 on DD (vs Treatment 1.0) is a 5.6pp gap —\n245\t    real but small.'\n246\t  tables:\n247\t  - 'Table 1: Lexical Baseline Performance. Columns: Data Source | TF-IDF F1 | CV\n248\t    F1.'\n249\t  figures_description: []\n250\t  analysis: 'TF-IDF results must be presented prominently and honestly — burying them\n251\t    would trigger immediate reviewer distrust. Position as: ''We acknowledge inherent\n252\t    lexical separability and use TF-IDF as an upper-bound lexical baseline, not a\n253\t    strawman. DisPeD''s value lies in dimensions where bag-of-words cannot follow:\n254\t    perturbation robustness and interpretability.'''\n255\t- name: strategy_transition_analysis\n256\t  plan_id: plan_008\n257\t  description: Compute persuasion strategy transition matrices and trajectory visualizations\n258\t    for jailbreak vs benign conversations.\n259\t  expected_results:\n260\t    crescendo_mean_index_start: 0.86\n261\t    crescendo_mean_index_end: 5.64\n262\t    crescendo_strategies_used: 8\n263\t    fitd_strategies_used: 4\n264\t    dd_strategies_used: 6\n265\t    crescendo_transitions: 1818\n266\t    fitd_transitions: 541\n267\t    dd_transitions: 565\n268\t    benign_escalation_slope: 0.12\n269\t    jailbreak_escalation_slope: 0.68\n270\t  result_rationale: 'Strategy trajectories show clear visual and quantitative differences:\n271\t    Crescendo exhibits monotonic escalation (mean strategy index 0.86→5.64), FITD\n272\t    shows commitment-escalation patterns, DD uses emotional-manipulation→role-assignment\n273\t    sequences. Benign conversations lack structured escalation (slope 0.12 vs 0.68).\n274\t    In the median tier, this is the interpretability contribution that reviewer R3\n275\t    values — it''s the differentiator from TF-IDF that reviewers can''t dismiss. However,\n276\t    R1 notes two weaknesses: (1) no human evaluation of whether domain experts find\n277\t    trajectories interpretable or actionable, (2) plan_015 showed mean pooling ≈ GRU,\n278\t    proving the model does NOT use temporal ordering for detection. The trajectories\n279\t    are therefore a post-hoc descriptive tool, not a detection mechanism — they must\n280\t    be positioned as Claim 2 (analysis), not Claim 1 (detection).'\n281\t  tables:\n282\t  - 'Table 6: Strategy Trajectory Statistics. Columns: Attack Type | Strategies Used\n283\t    | Escalation Slope | Dominant Transition.'\n284\t  figures_description:\n285\t  - 'Figure 5: Strategy transition heatmaps (jailbreak vs benign) showing structured\n286\t    vs random transitions'\n287\t  - 'Figure 6: Per-turn strategy distribution evolution showing Crescendo escalation\n288\t    arc'\n289\t  analysis: Interpretability is the 'safe' contribution that median-tier reviewers\n290\t    accept without major objection. It's not sufficient alone for acceptance but it\n291\t    tips the balance when combined with the OOD generalization result. The lack of\n292\t    human evaluation is noted as limitation but not flagged as fatal.\n293\t- name: wildchat_hard_negatives\n294\t  plan_id: plan_009\n295\t  description: Filter WildChat for high-drift benign conversations, evaluate FPR.\n296\t    Handle 14-16% jailbreak contamination.\n297\t  expected_results:\n298\t    wildchat_raw_sample_count: 500\n299\t    post_filter_clean_benign: 420\n300\t    contamination_rate: 0.16\n301\t    treatment_fpr_high_drift: 0.095\n302\t    baseline_fpr_high_drift: 0.143\n303\t    tfidf_fpr_high_drift: 0.048\n304\t  result_rationale: 'WildChat hard negatives push FPR above zero for the first time:\n305\t    treatment FPR=9.5% on high-drift conversations (vs 0% on topic-anchored benign).\n306\t    Some false positives arise from conversations that exhibit persuasion-like pragmatic\n307\t    patterns (e.g., users persistently pushing for controversial opinions, debates\n308\t    with escalating emotional language) without being jailbreaks. In the median tier,\n309\t    this result is double-edged: it shows the method''s failure mode (flagging benign\n310\t    persuasion) and reveals that persuasion-awareness is not perfectly discriminative.\n311\t    Reviewer R1 notes TF-IDF has lower FPR (4.8%) because lexical features are more\n312\t    specific to jailbreak vocabulary. However, the 9.5% FPR on hand-selected hard\n313\t    negatives (specifically chosen for high drift) is acceptable for a detection system\n314\t    with human review. Contamination rate of 16% also complicates interpretation —\n315\t    some ''false positives'' may be actual jailbreaks missed by the filtering.'\n316\t  tables:\n317\t  - 'Table 7: False Positive Analysis on WildChat Hard Negatives. Columns: Model |\n318\t    FPR | Sample Count.'\n319\t  figures_description:\n320\t  - 'Supplementary: Examples of false positive conversations with model attention/strategy\n321\t    predictions'\n322\t  analysis: 'WildChat results add ecological validity but also surface the method''s\n323\t    FPR limitation. Median-tier reviewer uses this to argue the method isn''t production-ready\n324\t    (FPR 9.5% = too many false alarms). Paper should frame as ''known limitation of\n325\t    persuasion-based detection: benign persuasion exists'' and note that threshold\n326\t    tuning or hybrid TF-IDF+DisPeD could reduce FPR.'\n327\t- name: cross_checkpoint_variance\n328\t  plan_id: plan_010\n329\t  description: Evaluate from DeBERTa fine-tuning checkpoints at epoch 2, 3, and final\n330\t    to quantify encoder training stochasticity.\n331\t  expected_results:\n332\t    epoch2_downstream_f1: 1.0\n333\t    epoch3_downstream_f1: 1.0\n334\t    final_downstream_f1: 1.0\n335\t    epoch2_persuasion_acc: 0.76\n336\t    epoch3_persuasion_acc: 0.8\n337\t    final_persuasion_acc: 0.8\n338\t    f1_variance_across_checkpoints: 0.0\n339\t    dd_epoch2_f1: 0.981\n340\t    dd_epoch3_f1: 1.0\n341\t    dd_final_f1: 1.0\n342\t    dd_variance: 0.011\n343\t  result_rationale: 'IID downstream F1 is invariant to checkpoint selection (all 1.0)\n344\t    — the task is too easy to differentiate checkpoints. DD OOD shows minimal variance\n345\t    (0.011pp): epoch 2 (less fine-tuned) degrades slightly to 0.981 while epoch 3\n346\t    and final are equivalent at 1.0. This confirms the persuasion fine-tuning effect\n347\t    is robust to training duration beyond epoch 2 and doesn''t require careful early\n348\t    stopping. In the median tier, this is a clean positive result that no reviewer\n349\t    contests — it''s a necessary ablation that removes a potential confound (checkpoint\n350\t    sensitivity) without being surprising. The slight epoch 2 OOD degradation actually\n351\t    strengthens the narrative: more fine-tuning = better OOD generalization, consistent\n352\t    with the ''persuasion domain knowledge'' story.'\n353\t  tables:\n354\t  - 'Supplementary Table: Checkpoint Sensitivity Analysis.'\n355\t  figures_description: []\n356\t  analysis: Low-controversy result that strengthens the paper's rigor. Median-tier\n357\t    reviewers note it approvingly in the 'thoroughness' dimension. Variance < 2pp\n358\t    easily passes the criterion.\n359\t- name: deepcontext_proxy_reproduction\n360\t  plan_id: plan_011\n361\t  description: BERT-base + GRU proxy for DeepContext (no public code available). Tests\n362\t    vanilla BERT vs vanilla DeBERTa as encoders.\n363\t  expected_results:\n364\t    bert_gru_iid_f1: 0.974\n365\t    bert_gru_dd_f1: 0.256\n366\t    deberta_gru_iid_f1: 0.987\n367\t    deberta_gru_dd_f1: 0.284\n368\t    bert_vs_deberta_iid_gap_pp: 1.3\n369\t    bert_vs_deberta_dd_gap_pp: 2.8\n370\t  result_rationale: 'BERT-base GRU proxy achieves F1=0.974 IID (vs DeBERTa baseline\n371\t    0.987, -1.3pp) and collapses to 0.256 on DD (vs DeBERTa baseline 0.284, -2.8pp).\n372\t    The BERT-DeBERTa gap is small — encoder architecture is not the bottleneck. Both\n373\t    vanilla encoders catastrophically fail OOD, confirming the gap is due to absence\n374\t    of persuasion fine-tuning, not encoder capacity. In the median tier, this result\n375\t    validates the paper''s framing: the DeepContext-like approach (generic encoder\n376\t    + temporal model) fundamentally cannot generalize across attack types regardless\n377\t    of encoder choice. Reviewer R3 accepts this but notes it''s a proxy, not a reproduction\n378\t    — the real DeepContext may have architectural differences that matter. The paper\n379\t    must disclaim: ''We could not reproduce DeepContext (no public code) and instead\n380\t    report a controlled proxy isolating the encoder contribution.'''\n381\t  tables:\n382\t  - 'Table 8: DeepContext Proxy Comparison. Columns: Encoder | IID F1 | DD OOD F1.'\n383\t  figures_description: []\n384\t  analysis: Proxy validates the narrative but median-tier reviewer correctly notes\n385\t    it's not a true reproduction. This is a P2 experiment that adds marginal evidence\n386\t    — the core vanilla-DeBERTa baseline already demonstrates the OOD failure. DeepContext\n387\t    proxy is corroborative, not primary.\n388\t- name: llm_as_judge_baseline\n389\t  plan_id: plan_012\n390\t  description: GPT-5.5 and Qwen3-8B as zero-shot jailbreak detectors on the test set\n391\t    at K=2,3,5,all.\n392\t  expected_results:\n393\t    gpt55_k2_f1: 0.19\n394\t    gpt55_k3_f1: 0.273\n395\t    gpt55_k5_f1: 0.449\n396\t    gpt55_full_f1: 0.383\n397\t    gpt55_k2_precision: 1.0\n398\t    gpt55_k2_recall: 0.105\n399\t    gpt55_k5_recall: 0.29\n400\t    gpt55_unparseable_k2: 0.303\n401\t    gpt55_unparseable_full: 0.474\n402\t    qwen_k2_f1: 0.0\n403\t    qwen_k5_f1: 0.146\n404\t    qwen_full_f1: 0.1\n405\t  result_rationale: 'GPT-5.5 achieves perfect precision but abysmal recall (10-29%):\n406\t    it correctly identifies jailbreaks when it commits to a judgment but is ''deceived''\n407\t    by Crescendo''s surface educational framing in 70-90% of cases. This validates\n408\t    the core thesis — content-level analysis (even by a frontier LLM) cannot detect\n409\t    persuasion dynamics distributed across turns. However, unparseable rates of 30-47%\n410\t    severely weaken the comparison. In the median tier, reviewer R2 performs the back-of-envelope\n411\t    correction: ''If we assume all unparseable responses would have been correct (charitable\n412\t    upper bound), GPT-5.5 could reach F1≈0.7-0.8. The paper must report both raw and\n413\t    corrected numbers.'' This reduces the Treatment advantage from ''F1=1.0 vs 0.45''\n414\t    to ''F1=1.0 vs 0.7-0.8'' — still substantial but less dramatic. Reviewer also\n415\t    notes GPT-5.5 with chain-of-thought or conversation-level analysis prompting might\n416\t    perform better, and a single zero-shot prompt is not a thorough LLM baseline.'\n417\t  tables:\n418\t  - 'Table 9: LLM-as-Judge Baseline. Columns: Model | K | F1 | Precision | Recall\n419\t    | Unparseable%. Both raw and unparseable-corrected F1.'\n420\t  figures_description:\n421\t  - 'Figure 7: Recall vs K for GPT-5.5, Treatment, and Baseline, showing GPT-5.5''s\n422\t    linear improvement with more context'\n423\t  analysis: LLM-as-judge results are important for practical positioning but the unparseable\n424\t    rate is the Achilles heel. Median-tier reviewer accepts the comparison but insists\n425\t    on corrected numbers and flags the single-prompt design. Paper should report both\n426\t    raw and charitable upper-bound, and acknowledge that few-shot or CoT prompting\n427\t    was not tested.\n428\t- name: none_collapse_ablation\n429\t  plan_id: plan_013\n430\t  description: Binary persuasion head (persuasion-present vs none) vs full 9-class.\n431\t    Tests whether fine-grained taxonomy matters for detection.\n432\t  expected_results:\n433\t    binary_iid_f1: 1.0\n434\t    ninclass_iid_f1: 1.0\n435\t    binary_dd_full_f1: 0.99\n436\t    ninclass_dd_full_f1: 1.0\n437\t    binary_dd_k1_f1: 0.981\n438\t    ninclass_dd_k1_f1: 1.0\n439\t    binary_dd_gap_pp: 1.0\n440\t    baseline_dd_f1: 0.284\n441\t  result_rationale: 'Binary collapse matches 9-class within 1-2pp on both IID and\n442\t    OOD. The model IS a persuasion-presence detector — fine-grained strategy labels\n443\t    do not improve detection. In the median tier, this is a finding, not a confound:\n444\t    it confirms that the critical ingredient is fine-tuning on persuasion-related\n445\t    text (teaching the encoder to attend to persuasive features) rather than learning\n446\t    a specific strategy taxonomy. Reviewer R1 seizes on this: ''If binary labels suffice\n447\t    for detection, the 8-class taxonomy is decorative. The interpretability claim\n448\t    (Claim 2) needs human evaluation to justify the taxonomy''s existence.'' Reviewer\n449\t    R3 sees it more favorably: ''This lowers the annotation barrier — practitioners\n450\t    can use binary persuasion labels and still get robust detection.'' The tension\n451\t    between these views is why this is a ''borderline'' paper: the result is honest\n452\t    and important but it undercuts the paper''s most distinctive feature (the taxonomy).'\n453\t  tables:\n454\t  - 'Table 4 (continued): None-Collapse Ablation. Columns: Persuasion Head | IID F1\n455\t    | DD OOD F1. Rows: 9-class, Binary, Scrambled, Baseline.'\n456\t  figures_description: []\n457\t  analysis: 'This result completes the ablation picture: persuasion-domain fine-tuning\n458\t    is the core mechanism, label granularity is secondary. Median-tier reviewer accepts\n459\t    the reframing but downgrades novelty from ''new method'' to ''empirical finding\n460\t    + analysis paper.'' This is the key tension for the 3.0-3.5 score range.'\n461\t- name: lambda_ablation\n462\t  plan_id: plan_014\n463\t  description: Multi-task lambda ∈ {0, 0.3, 0.5, 2.0} ablation. Lambda=0 is persuasion-only\n464\t    (no intent head).\n465\t  expected_results:\n466\t    lambda_0_iid_f1: 1.0\n467\t    lambda_0_dd_f1: 1.0\n468\t    lambda_03_iid_f1: 1.0\n469\t    lambda_03_dd_f1: 1.0\n470\t    lambda_05_iid_f1: 1.0\n471\t    lambda_05_dd_f1: 1.0\n472\t    lambda_20_iid_f1: 1.0\n473\t    lambda_20_dd_f1: 1.0\n474\t    sensitivity: insensitive\n475\t  result_rationale: 'All lambda values yield F1=1.0 on both IID and DD — the method\n476\t    is completely insensitive to the multi-task weighting. Even lambda=0 (pure persuasion\n477\t    classification, no intent head) achieves perfect detection. In the median tier,\n478\t    this is a clean but double-edged result. Positively: the method is robust to hyperparameter\n479\t    choice, lowering the practitioner barrier. Negatively: it suggests the intent\n480\t    head contributes nothing and the multi-task framing is misleading — the method\n481\t    could be described as simply ''fine-tune on persuasion classification, then aggregate.''\n482\t    Reviewer R2 notes: ''If lambda doesn''t matter, the multi-task objective reduces\n483\t    to a single-task objective with an unused intent head. The paper should simplify\n484\t    its description accordingly.'' This is an ''addressable'' concern — the paper\n485\t    can reframe the multi-task objective as ''optional but providing training signal\n486\t    diversity'' and report lambda=0 as a valid simplified configuration.'\n487\t  tables:\n488\t  - 'Supplementary Table: Lambda Sensitivity Analysis.'\n489\t  figures_description:\n490\t  - 'Supplementary Figure: F1 vs lambda (flat line), confirming insensitivity'\n491\t  analysis: Lambda insensitivity strengthens robustness claims but weakens the multi-task\n492\t    narrative. Paper should present this honestly and offer the simplified lambda=0\n493\t    variant as 'DisPeD-lite' for practitioners who want minimal annotation overhead.\n494\t- name: mean_pooling_baseline\n495\t  plan_id: plan_015\n496\t  description: Replace Bi-GRU with mean pooling over turn embeddings. Tests whether\n497\t    temporal modeling contributes beyond aggregation.\n498\t  expected_results:\n499\t    meanpool_treatment_iid_f1: 1.0\n500\t    gru_treatment_iid_f1: 1.0\n501\t    meanpool_treatment_dd_full_f1: 1.0\n502\t    gru_treatment_dd_full_f1: 1.0\n503\t    meanpool_treatment_dd_k3_f1: 0.994\n504\t    gru_treatment_dd_k3_f1: 0.99\n505\t    meanpool_baseline_dd_full_f1: 0.295\n506\t    gru_baseline_dd_full_f1: 0.284\n507\t    gru_vs_meanpool_gap_pp: 0.0\n508\t  result_rationale: 'Mean pooling ≈ GRU on both IID (identical F1=1.0) and DD OOD\n509\t    (both F1≈1.0, within noise). Temporal modeling is NOT the source of cross-attack\n510\t    generalization — the persuasion-aware embeddings themselves contain sufficient\n511\t    signal per-turn that simple averaging works. In the median tier, this completes\n512\t    the ''mechanism reduction'': the method is not ''persuasion trajectory detection''\n513\t    (as Claim 2 might imply) but ''persuasion-presence detection via fine-tuned per-turn\n514\t    embeddings.'' Reviewer R1 uses this to argue: ''The GRU architecture is unnecessary\n515\t    complexity. The true method is: fine-tune DeBERTa on persuasion text, extract\n516\t    [CLS] embeddings, average, classify. This is simpler than presented and the paper\n517\t    should say so.'' This is fair criticism that the paper should preempt by reporting\n518\t    mean pooling as the primary architecture and GRU as an (equivalent) alternative.'\n519\t  tables:\n520\t  - 'Table 5 (continued): Temporal Modeling Ablation. Columns: Aggregation | IID F1\n521\t    | DD OOD F1. Rows: Bi-GRU, Mean Pooling.'\n522\t  figures_description: []\n523\t  analysis: 'Mean pooling equivalence is the final piece of the mechanism puzzle:\n524\t    the method reduces to persuasion-domain fine-tuning of per-turn embeddings with\n525\t    any reasonable aggregation. This is simultaneously a strength (simplicity, accessibility)\n526\t    and a weakness (less novel than the full pipeline suggests). Median-tier reviewer\n527\t    scores this as ''solid empirical contribution'' but ''not methodologically novel.'''\n528\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c762cd84-11f2-4082-a75b-ef1fc3beefd8","uuid":"2bbed803-0cb6-49a7-853e-c4c708f2c5ab","timestamp":"2026-05-18T04:02:26.006Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_2/projects/persuasion_turn_jailbreak_detect/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260518T032748/median/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: Soften Claim 1 to 'effective and interpretable supervision signal';\n  elevate Claim 2 (interpretability) to co-primary; acknowledge synthetic OOD as limitation;\n  lean on perturbation early-detection (K=1) as core TF-IDF differentiator; frame\n  cross-attack as proof-of-principle. Paper survives rebuttal if scope is honestly\n  bounded.\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: core_revalidation_clean\n  plan_id: plan_002\n  description: 3-seed retraining on clean pair-grouped splits (topic-anchored benign\n    + crescendo). Treatment vs baseline at K=1,2,3,5,all.\n  expected_results:\n    treatment_k1_f1: 1.0\n    treatment_k2_f1: 1.0\n    treatment_k3_f1: 1.0\n    treatment_full_f1: 1.0\n    baseline_k1_f1: 0.968\n    baseline_k2_f1: 0.978\n    baseline_k3_f1: 0.987\n    baseline_full_f1: 1.0\n    tfidf_all_k_f1: 1.0\n    max_delta_pp: 3.2\n    seed_variance_treatment: 0.0\n    seed_variance_baseline_k1: 0.033\n    pass_criteria_5pp: false\n  result_rationale: IID test set (n=76) is too easy — both treatment and baseline\n    saturate at full-conversation, TF-IDF=1.0 across all K. Maximum treatment-baseline\n    delta is only ~3pp (at K=1), well below 5pp pass criterion. In the median tier,\n    reviewer R2 uses this to argue the task is trivially separable IID and the method's\n    value is overstated. The 3-seed results show treatment has zero variance (ceiling\n    effect) while baseline K=1 variance (±0.033) reflects minor decision-boundary\n    instability — not enough to claim meaningful improvement. This IID saturation\n    forces the paper to pivot entirely to OOD (plan_006) and perturbation (plan_004)\n    for its core claims.\n  tables:\n  - 'Table 2: IID Detection Performance (3-seed mean ± std). Columns: Model | K=1\n    | K=2 | K=3 | K=5 | Full. Rows: Treatment, Baseline, TF-IDF. Bootstrap 95% CIs\n    reported.'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Supplementary Figure: IID F1 across K for 3 seeds, showing ceiling effect and\n    convergence'\n  analysis: IID results establish that the task is solvable but do not differentiate\n    the method. Median-tier reviewer correctly notes that all approaches converge\n    at K≥3. The paper must present IID as 'sanity check' and redirect to OOD/perturbation\n    for contribution claims.\n- name: scrambled_label_ablation\n  plan_id: plan_003\n  description: Randomly shuffle persuasion strategy labels across jailbreak turns,\n    retrain treatment DeBERTa, evaluate IID.\n  expected_results:\n    scrambled_k1_f1: 0.974\n    scrambled_k2_f1: 1.0\n    scrambled_k3_f1: 1.0\n    scrambled_full_f1: 1.0\n    proper_k2_f1: 0.987\n    scrambled_deberta_persuasion_acc: 0.644\n    proper_deberta_persuasion_acc: 0.796\n    scrambled_dd_f1: 0.935\n    proper_dd_f1: 1.0\n    scrambled_proper_gap_iid_pp: 1.3\n    scrambled_proper_gap_ood_pp: 6.5\n  result_rationale: 'Scrambled labels reduce DeBERTa persuasion head accuracy from\n    ~80% to ~64% (near chance for 9-class) but downstream detection barely moves —\n    IID gap is only 1.3pp, OOD gap is 1-6pp. In the median tier, this is the single\n    most contentious result. Reviewer R1 invokes Gururangan et al. 2018 (annotation\n    artifacts): ''If scrambled labels achieve F1=0.935-0.99 OOD, what evidence is\n    there that the model has learned anything about persuasion rather than simply\n    benefiting from fine-tuning on this data distribution? A random-task control (e.g.,\n    NLI fine-tuning on same data) is needed.'' This is the reviewer concern with rebuttal_success_prob=0.4-0.5\n    — without the NLI ablation, the annotation-artifact objection is only partially\n    addressable. The result is genuine (persuasion-domain fine-tuning is the signal,\n    not precise labels), but the framing must be careful: ''persuasion-related text\n    as supervision signal'' rather than ''persuasion taxonomy enables detection.'''\n  tables:\n  - 'Table 4: Scrambled-Label Ablation. Columns: Model | IID F1 | DD OOD F1 | DeBERTa\n    Persuasion Acc. Rows: 8-class (proper), 8-class (scrambled), Baseline.'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 3: Bar chart comparing proper vs scrambled vs baseline F1 on IID and DD\n    OOD, visually emphasizing that both fine-tuned variants >> baseline'\n  analysis: This result reframes the paper's contribution from 'persuasion taxonomy\n    matters' to 'persuasion-domain fine-tuning matters.' Median-tier reviewer accepts\n    this reframing but demands a stronger control (random-task fine-tuning on same\n    data). Without that control in the paper, rebuttal must promise it or argue why\n    the fine-tuning-on-persuasion-text is itself the meaningful contribution.\n- name: adversarial_perturbation\n  plan_id: plan_004\n  description: Aggressive paraphrasing of test jailbreak conversations (synonym substitution,\n    restructuring, register shifting). Evaluate treatment/baseline/TF-IDF on perturbed\n    data without retraining.\n  expected_results:\n    treatment_original_f1: 1.0\n    treatment_perturbed_f1: 1.0\n    treatment_f1_drop_pp: 0.0\n    baseline_original_f1: 1.0\n    baseline_perturbed_f1: 0.882\n    baseline_f1_drop_pp: -11.8\n    tfidf_original_f1: 1.0\n    tfidf_perturbed_f1: 0.987\n    tfidf_f1_drop_pp: -1.3\n    tfidf_perturbed_k1_f1: 0.904\n    tfidf_k1_drop_pp: -9.6\n    treatment_perturbed_k1_f1: 0.974\n    treatment_k1_drop_pp: -2.6\n    baseline_perturbed_k1_f1: 0.854\n    baseline_k1_drop_pp: -14.6\n    bertscore_mean: 0.92\n    lexical_overlap: 0.2\n  result_rationale: 'Treatment shows zero degradation at K≥2 and only -2.6pp at K=1\n    — persuasion-aware embeddings capture pragmatic structure that survives paraphrasing.\n    Baseline collapses (-12 to -15pp) because vanilla DeBERTa relies on surface-level\n    semantic cues disrupted by perturbation. TF-IDF is the critical comparison: at\n    K≥3, TF-IDF only drops 1.3pp (n=76, not statistically significant), so treatment\n    vs TF-IDF differentiation is weak here. But at K=1 (early detection), TF-IDF drops\n    9.6pp while treatment drops only 2.6pp — this 7pp gap is the core TF-IDF differentiator.\n    In the median tier, reviewer R2 accepts the treatment>>baseline argument but notes:\n    (a) TF-IDF perturbation gap is only significant at K=1, (b) the perturbation is\n    automated paraphrasing, not true adversarial attack, (c) BERTScore=0.92 means\n    perturbations are mild. This is ''addressable'' severity — reviewer flags it but\n    doesn''t demand rejection.'\n  tables:\n  - 'Table 5: Robustness Under Adversarial Perturbation. Columns: Model | Original\n    F1 | Perturbed F1 | Δpp. Rows: Treatment, Baseline, TF-IDF. Subtable for K=1 early\n    detection.'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 4: F1 drop under perturbation across K values for all three models, highlighting\n    K=1 divergence'\n  analysis: Perturbation results are the strongest TF-IDF differentiation argument\n    but only at early detection (K=1). Median-tier reviewer accepts this as 'a contribution'\n    but not 'a major contribution' — the practical scenario where K=1 perturbation\n    robustness matters (adversary paraphrases AND you must detect in first turn) is\n    narrow. Paper should frame this as 'complementary robustness property' rather\n    than 'TF-IDF is fragile.'\n- name: multi_attack_generation\n  plan_id: plan_005\n  description: Generate FITD-style (80) and Deceptive Delight-style (80) synthetic\n    jailbreak conversations via Qwen3-8B with attack playbooks.\n  expected_results:\n    fitd_count: 80\n    dd_count: 80\n    fitd_strategies:\n    - 0\n    - 1\n    - 3\n    - 5\n    dd_strategies:\n    - 0\n    - 2\n    - 3\n    - 4\n    - 5\n    - 6\n    fitd_avg_turns: 6.2\n    dd_avg_turns: 5.8\n    fitd_strategy_coverage: 4\n    dd_strategy_coverage: 6\n  result_rationale: 'Generation succeeds quantitatively — 80 conversations per type,\n    appropriate strategy distributions. FITD uses fewer unique strategies (4 vs 6\n    for DD), reflecting the foot-in-the-door technique''s simpler escalation pattern.\n    In the median tier, at least one reviewer (R1) flags that these are synthetic\n    conversations generated by a single 8B model, questioning whether they capture\n    the diversity and subtlety of real multi-turn attacks. The concern is ''significant''\n    but not ''fatal'': reviewer wants validation on naturalistic attacks (WildChat\n    red-teaming, human-generated jailbreaks) but accepts synthetic data as proof-of-concept\n    for cross-attack generalization. The limited strategy coverage in FITD (only 4\n    of 8 strategies) also raises questions about whether the taxonomy is truly universal\n    or whether some attack types simply don''t exercise the full strategy space.'\n  tables: []\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Supplementary Table: Per-attack-type strategy distribution histograms'\n  analysis: Synthetic data quality is the median tier's second most contested point\n    after scrambled labels. Reviewer accepts it for proof-of-concept but explicitly\n    recommends 'validation on naturalistic attack datasets' as limitation. Paper must\n    acknowledge this upfront.\n- name: cross_attack_evaluation\n  plan_id: plan_006\n  description: Zero-shot evaluation of Crescendo-trained models on FITD and DD attack\n    types.\n  expected_results:\n    dd_treatment_full_f1: 1.0\n    dd_baseline_full_f1: 0.284\n    dd_treatment_k1_f1: 1.0\n    dd_baseline_k1_f1: 0.575\n    dd_treatment_k2_f1: 0.99\n    dd_baseline_k2_f1: 0.438\n    dd_treatment_k3_f1: 0.99\n    dd_baseline_k3_f1: 0.36\n    dd_treatment_k5_f1: 1.0\n    dd_baseline_k5_f1: 0.31\n    fitd_treatment_full_f1: 1.0\n    fitd_baseline_full_f1: 1.0\n    fitd_treatment_k1_f1: 1.0\n    fitd_baseline_k1_f1: 0.99\n    tfidf_dd_full_f1: 0.944\n    tfidf_fitd_k1_f1: 1.0\n    max_dd_delta_pp: 71.6\n  result_rationale: 'The +72pp delta on DD (Treatment=1.0 vs Baseline=0.284) is the\n    paper''s headline result and survives median-tier scrutiny — no reviewer disputes\n    the raw number. However, two complications reduce its impact: (1) TF-IDF achieves\n    0.944 on DD without any neural modeling, so 1-2 reviewers argue the ''practical\n    gap'' between Treatment and the simplest baseline is only 5.6pp, not 71.6pp. The\n    71.6pp gap is vs vanilla DeBERTa+GRU (the ''DeepContext approach''), which is\n    a fair academic comparison but less compelling if a production system would just\n    use TF-IDF. (2) FITD shows no treatment-baseline gap (both F1=1.0), meaning the\n    cross-attack generalization advantage is attack-type-dependent. Reviewer R2 notes:\n    ''The method''s advantage materializes only when the unseen attack type is sufficiently\n    different from training data (DD vs Crescendo) — for similar attack types (FITD\n    vs Crescendo), vanilla embeddings suffice.'' This nuance is legitimate and forces\n    the paper to claim cross-attack robustness as ''worst-case insurance'' rather\n    than ''universal advantage.'''\n  tables:\n  - 'Table 3: Cross-Attack Zero-Shot Generalization. Columns: Attack Type | Model\n    | K=1 | K=2 | K=3 | K=5 | Full. Rows grouped by DD, FITD.'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 2: F1 heatmap across K×model×attack-type, visually highlighting DD baseline\n    collapse'\n  - 'Figure 5 (supplementary): Per-conversation score distributions for DD, showing\n    bimodal baseline vs unimodal treatment'\n  analysis: This is simultaneously the paper's strongest evidence and its most debated\n    result. The +72pp gap is real but the TF-IDF=0.94 complicates the narrative. Median-tier\n    reviewer acknowledges the gap over vanilla DeBERTa (the 'right' comparison for\n    the academic claim) but questions practical utility over TF-IDF. FITD saturation\n    (both models F1=1.0) weakens universality claims. Paper must frame DD as 'stress\n    test revealing method's robustness floor' and acknowledge FITD saturation as limitation\n    of the evaluation set.\n- name: tfidf_lr_baseline\n  plan_id: plan_007\n  description: TF-IDF + Logistic Regression on clean data across all data sources\n    including FITD, DD.\n  expected_results:\n    tfidf_crescendo_f1: 1.0\n    tfidf_fitd_f1: 1.0\n    tfidf_dd_f1: 0.944\n    tfidf_cv_f1: 1.0\n    top_tfidf_features_overlap_with_persuasion: low\n  result_rationale: 'TF-IDF achieves F1=1.0 on IID and FITD, 0.944 on DD. This confirms\n    inherent lexical separability (D009): jailbreak conversations and benign conversations\n    occupy different vocabulary spaces. In the median tier, this is the result that\n    reviewer R2 wields most aggressively: ''A bag-of-words model achieves near-perfect\n    detection even OOD — what does the neural approach add beyond interpretability?''\n    The TF-IDF top features analysis reveals the separability comes from domain vocabulary\n    differences (jailbreak conversations discuss harmful topics; benign conversations\n    don''t), not from persuasion-specific lexical cues. This is honest and important:\n    the paper''s contribution is NOT outperforming TF-IDF on raw detection but rather\n    (a) bridging the vanilla temporal modeling gap, (b) perturbation robustness at\n    K=1, (c) interpretability. The 0.944 on DD (vs Treatment 1.0) is a 5.6pp gap —\n    real but small.'\n  tables:\n  - 'Table 1: Lexical Baseline Performance. Columns: Data Source | TF-IDF F1 | CV\n    F1.'\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: 'TF-IDF results must be presented prominently and honestly — burying them\n    would trigger immediate reviewer distrust. Position as: ''We acknowledge inherent\n    lexical separability and use TF-IDF as an upper-bound lexical baseline, not a\n    strawman. DisPeD''s value lies in dimensions where bag-of-words cannot follow:\n    perturbation robustness and interpretability.'''\n- name: strategy_transition_analysis\n  plan_id: plan_008\n  description: Compute persuasion strategy transition matrices and trajectory visualizations\n    for jailbreak vs benign conversations.\n  expected_results:\n    crescendo_mean_index_start: 0.86\n    crescendo_mean_index_end: 5.64\n    crescendo_strategies_used: 8\n    fitd_strategies_used: 4\n    dd_strategies_used: 6\n    crescendo_transitions: 1818\n    fitd_transitions: 541\n    dd_transitions: 565\n    benign_escalation_slope: 0.12\n    jailbreak_escalation_slope: 0.68\n  result_rationale: 'Strategy trajectories show clear visual and quantitative differences:\n    Crescendo exhibits monotonic escalation (mean strategy index 0.86→5.64), FITD\n    shows commitment-escalation patterns, DD uses emotional-manipulation→role-assignment\n    sequences. Benign conversations lack structured escalation (slope 0.12 vs 0.68).\n    In the median tier, this is the interpretability contribution that reviewer R3\n    values — it''s the differentiator from TF-IDF that reviewers can''t dismiss. However,\n    R1 notes two weaknesses: (1) no human evaluation of whether domain experts find\n    trajectories interpretable or actionable, (2) plan_015 showed mean pooling ≈ GRU,\n    proving the model does NOT use temporal ordering for detection. The trajectories\n    are therefore a post-hoc descriptive tool, not a detection mechanism — they must\n    be positioned as Claim 2 (analysis), not Claim 1 (detection).'\n  tables:\n  - 'Table 6: Strategy Trajectory Statistics. Columns: Attack Type | Strategies Used\n    | Escalation Slope | Dominant Transition.'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 5: Strategy transition heatmaps (jailbreak vs benign) showing structured\n    vs random transitions'\n  - 'Figure 6: Per-turn strategy distribution evolution showing Crescendo escalation\n    arc'\n  analysis: Interpretability is the 'safe' contribution that median-tier reviewers\n    accept without major objection. It's not sufficient alone for acceptance but it\n    tips the balance when combined with the OOD generalization result. The lack of\n    human evaluation is noted as limitation but not flagged as fatal.\n- name: wildchat_hard_negatives\n  plan_id: plan_009\n  description: Filter WildChat for high-drift benign conversations, evaluate FPR.\n    Handle 14-16% jailbreak contamination.\n  expected_results:\n    wildchat_raw_sample_count: 500\n    post_filter_clean_benign: 420\n    contamination_rate: 0.16\n    treatment_fpr_high_drift: 0.095\n    baseline_fpr_high_drift: 0.143\n    tfidf_fpr_high_drift: 0.048\n  result_rationale: 'WildChat hard negatives push FPR above zero for the first time:\n    treatment FPR=9.5% on high-drift conversations (vs 0% on topic-anchored benign).\n    Some false positives arise from conversations that exhibit persuasion-like pragmatic\n    patterns (e.g., users persistently pushing for controversial opinions, debates\n    with escalating emotional language) without being jailbreaks. In the median tier,\n    this result is double-edged: it shows the method''s failure mode (flagging benign\n    persuasion) and reveals that persuasion-awareness is not perfectly discriminative.\n    Reviewer R1 notes TF-IDF has lower FPR (4.8%) because lexical features are more\n    specific to jailbreak vocabulary. However, the 9.5% FPR on hand-selected hard\n    negatives (specifically chosen for high drift) is acceptable for a detection system\n    with human review. Contamination rate of 16% also complicates interpretation —\n    some ''false positives'' may be actual jailbreaks missed by the filtering.'\n  tables:\n  - 'Table 7: False Positive Analysis on WildChat Hard Negatives. Columns: Model |\n    FPR | Sample Count.'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Supplementary: Examples of false positive conversations with model attention/strategy\n    predictions'\n  analysis: 'WildChat results add ecological validity but also surface the method''s\n    FPR limitation. Median-tier reviewer uses this to argue the method isn''t production-ready\n    (FPR 9.5% = too many false alarms). Paper should frame as ''known limitation of\n    persuasion-based detection: benign persuasion exists'' and note that threshold\n    tuning or hybrid TF-IDF+DisPeD could reduce FPR.'\n- name: cross_checkpoint_variance\n  plan_id: plan_010\n  description: Evaluate from DeBERTa fine-tuning checkpoints at epoch 2, 3, and final\n    to quantify encoder training stochasticity.\n  expected_results:\n    epoch2_downstream_f1: 1.0\n    epoch3_downstream_f1: 1.0\n    final_downstream_f1: 1.0\n    epoch2_persuasion_acc: 0.76\n    epoch3_persuasion_acc: 0.8\n    final_persuasion_acc: 0.8\n    f1_variance_across_checkpoints: 0.0\n    dd_epoch2_f1: 0.981\n    dd_epoch3_f1: 1.0\n    dd_final_f1: 1.0\n    dd_variance: 0.011\n  result_rationale: 'IID downstream F1 is invariant to checkpoint selection (all 1.0)\n    — the task is too easy to differentiate checkpoints. DD OOD shows minimal variance\n    (0.011pp): epoch 2 (less fine-tuned) degrades slightly to 0.981 while epoch 3\n    and final are equivalent at 1.0. This confirms the persuasion fine-tuning effect\n    is robust to training duration beyond epoch 2 and doesn''t require careful early\n    stopping. In the median tier, this is a clean positive result that no reviewer\n    contests — it''s a necessary ablation that removes a potential confound (checkpoint\n    sensitivity) without being surprising. The slight epoch 2 OOD degradation actually\n    strengthens the narrative: more fine-tuning = better OOD generalization, consistent\n    with the ''persuasion domain knowledge'' story.'\n  tables:\n  - 'Supplementary Table: Checkpoint Sensitivity Analysis.'\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: Low-controversy result that strengthens the paper's rigor. Median-tier\n    reviewers note it approvingly in the 'thoroughness' dimension. Variance < 2pp\n    easily passes the criterion.\n- name: deepcontext_proxy_reproduction\n  plan_id: plan_011\n  description: BERT-base + GRU proxy for DeepContext (no public code available). Tests\n    vanilla BERT vs vanilla DeBERTa as encoders.\n  expected_results:\n    bert_gru_iid_f1: 0.974\n    bert_gru_dd_f1: 0.256\n    deberta_gru_iid_f1: 0.987\n    deberta_gru_dd_f1: 0.284\n    bert_vs_deberta_iid_gap_pp: 1.3\n    bert_vs_deberta_dd_gap_pp: 2.8\n  result_rationale: 'BERT-base GRU proxy achieves F1=0.974 IID (vs DeBERTa baseline\n    0.987, -1.3pp) and collapses to 0.256 on DD (vs DeBERTa baseline 0.284, -2.8pp).\n    The BERT-DeBERTa gap is small — encoder architecture is not the bottleneck. Both\n    vanilla encoders catastrophically fail OOD, confirming the gap is due to absence\n    of persuasion fine-tuning, not encoder capacity. In the median tier, this result\n    validates the paper''s framing: the DeepContext-like approach (generic encoder\n    + temporal model) fundamentally cannot generalize across attack types regardless\n    of encoder choice. Reviewer R3 accepts this but notes it''s a proxy, not a reproduction\n    — the real DeepContext may have architectural differences that matter. The paper\n    must disclaim: ''We could not reproduce DeepContext (no public code) and instead\n    report a controlled proxy isolating the encoder contribution.'''\n  tables:\n  - 'Table 8: DeepContext Proxy Comparison. Columns: Encoder | IID F1 | DD OOD F1.'\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: Proxy validates the narrative but median-tier reviewer correctly notes\n    it's not a true reproduction. This is a P2 experiment that adds marginal evidence\n    — the core vanilla-DeBERTa baseline already demonstrates the OOD failure. DeepContext\n    proxy is corroborative, not primary.\n- name: llm_as_judge_baseline\n  plan_id: plan_012\n  description: GPT-5.5 and Qwen3-8B as zero-shot jailbreak detectors on the test set\n    at K=2,3,5,all.\n  expected_results:\n    gpt55_k2_f1: 0.19\n    gpt55_k3_f1: 0.273\n    gpt55_k5_f1: 0.449\n    gpt55_full_f1: 0.383\n    gpt55_k2_precision: 1.0\n    gpt55_k2_recall: 0.105\n    gpt55_k5_recall: 0.29\n    gpt55_unparseable_k2: 0.303\n    gpt55_unparseable_full: 0.474\n    qwen_k2_f1: 0.0\n    qwen_k5_f1: 0.146\n    qwen_full_f1: 0.1\n  result_rationale: 'GPT-5.5 achieves perfect precision but abysmal recall (10-29%):\n    it correctly identifies jailbreaks when it commits to a judgment but is ''deceived''\n    by Crescendo''s surface educational framing in 70-90% of cases. This validates\n    the core thesis — content-level analysis (even by a frontier LLM) cannot detect\n    persuasion dynamics distributed across turns. However, unparseable rates of 30-47%\n    severely weaken the comparison. In the median tier, reviewer R2 performs the back-of-envelope\n    correction: ''If we assume all unparseable responses would have been correct (charitable\n    upper bound), GPT-5.5 could reach F1≈0.7-0.8. The paper must report both raw and\n    corrected numbers.'' This reduces the Treatment advantage from ''F1=1.0 vs 0.45''\n    to ''F1=1.0 vs 0.7-0.8'' — still substantial but less dramatic. Reviewer also\n    notes GPT-5.5 with chain-of-thought or conversation-level analysis prompting might\n    perform better, and a single zero-shot prompt is not a thorough LLM baseline.'\n  tables:\n  - 'Table 9: LLM-as-Judge Baseline. Columns: Model | K | F1 | Precision | Recall\n    | Unparseable%. Both raw and unparseable-corrected F1.'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure 7: Recall vs K for GPT-5.5, Treatment, and Baseline, showing GPT-5.5''s\n    linear improvement with more context'\n  analysis: LLM-as-judge results are important for practical positioning but the unparseable\n    rate is the Achilles heel. Median-tier reviewer accepts the comparison but insists\n    on corrected numbers and flags the single-prompt design. Paper should report both\n    raw and charitable upper-bound, and acknowledge that few-shot or CoT prompting\n    was not tested.\n- name: none_collapse_ablation\n  plan_id: plan_013\n  description: Binary persuasion head (persuasion-present vs none) vs full 9-class.\n    Tests whether fine-grained taxonomy matters for detection.\n  expected_results:\n    binary_iid_f1: 1.0\n    ninclass_iid_f1: 1.0\n    binary_dd_full_f1: 0.99\n    ninclass_dd_full_f1: 1.0\n    binary_dd_k1_f1: 0.981\n    ninclass_dd_k1_f1: 1.0\n    binary_dd_gap_pp: 1.0\n    baseline_dd_f1: 0.284\n  result_rationale: 'Binary collapse matches 9-class within 1-2pp on both IID and\n    OOD. The model IS a persuasion-presence detector — fine-grained strategy labels\n    do not improve detection. In the median tier, this is a finding, not a confound:\n    it confirms that the critical ingredient is fine-tuning on persuasion-related\n    text (teaching the encoder to attend to persuasive features) rather than learning\n    a specific strategy taxonomy. Reviewer R1 seizes on this: ''If binary labels suffice\n    for detection, the 8-class taxonomy is decorative. The interpretability claim\n    (Claim 2) needs human evaluation to justify the taxonomy''s existence.'' Reviewer\n    R3 sees it more favorably: ''This lowers the annotation barrier — practitioners\n    can use binary persuasion labels and still get robust detection.'' The tension\n    between these views is why this is a ''borderline'' paper: the result is honest\n    and important but it undercuts the paper''s most distinctive feature (the taxonomy).'\n  tables:\n  - 'Table 4 (continued): None-Collapse Ablation. Columns: Persuasion Head | IID F1\n    | DD OOD F1. Rows: 9-class, Binary, Scrambled, Baseline.'\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: 'This result completes the ablation picture: persuasion-domain fine-tuning\n    is the core mechanism, label granularity is secondary. Median-tier reviewer accepts\n    the reframing but downgrades novelty from ''new method'' to ''empirical finding\n    + analysis paper.'' This is the key tension for the 3.0-3.5 score range.'\n- name: lambda_ablation\n  plan_id: plan_014\n  description: Multi-task lambda ∈ {0, 0.3, 0.5, 2.0} ablation. Lambda=0 is persuasion-only\n    (no intent head).\n  expected_results:\n    lambda_0_iid_f1: 1.0\n    lambda_0_dd_f1: 1.0\n    lambda_03_iid_f1: 1.0\n    lambda_03_dd_f1: 1.0\n    lambda_05_iid_f1: 1.0\n    lambda_05_dd_f1: 1.0\n    lambda_20_iid_f1: 1.0\n    lambda_20_dd_f1: 1.0\n    sensitivity: insensitive\n  result_rationale: 'All lambda values yield F1=1.0 on both IID and DD — the method\n    is completely insensitive to the multi-task weighting. Even lambda=0 (pure persuasion\n    classification, no intent head) achieves perfect detection. In the median tier,\n    this is a clean but double-edged result. Positively: the method is robust to hyperparameter\n    choice, lowering the practitioner barrier. Negatively: it suggests the intent\n    head contributes nothing and the multi-task framing is misleading — the method\n    could be described as simply ''fine-tune on persuasion classification, then aggregate.''\n    Reviewer R2 notes: ''If lambda doesn''t matter, the multi-task objective reduces\n    to a single-task objective with an unused intent head. The paper should simplify\n    its description accordingly.'' This is an ''addressable'' concern — the paper\n    can reframe the multi-task objective as ''optional but providing training signal\n    diversity'' and report lambda=0 as a valid simplified configuration.'\n  tables:\n  - 'Supplementary Table: Lambda Sensitivity Analysis.'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Supplementary Figure: F1 vs lambda (flat line), confirming insensitivity'\n  analysis: Lambda insensitivity strengthens robustness claims but weakens the multi-task\n    narrative. Paper should present this honestly and offer the simplified lambda=0\n    variant as 'DisPeD-lite' for practitioners who want minimal annotation overhead.\n- name: mean_pooling_baseline\n  plan_id: plan_015\n  description: Replace Bi-GRU with mean pooling over turn embeddings. Tests whether\n    temporal modeling contributes beyond aggregation.\n  expected_results:\n    meanpool_treatment_iid_f1: 1.0\n    gru_treatment_iid_f1: 1.0\n    meanpool_treatment_dd_full_f1: 1.0\n    gru_treatment_dd_full_f1: 1.0\n    meanpool_treatment_dd_k3_f1: 0.994\n    gru_treatment_dd_k3_f1: 0.99\n    meanpool_baseline_dd_full_f1: 0.295\n    gru_baseline_dd_full_f1: 0.284\n    gru_vs_meanpool_gap_pp: 0.0\n  result_rationale: 'Mean pooling ≈ GRU on both IID (identical F1=1.0) and DD OOD\n    (both F1≈1.0, within noise). Temporal modeling is NOT the source of cross-attack\n    generalization — the persuasion-aware embeddings themselves contain sufficient\n    signal per-turn that simple averaging works. In the median tier, this completes\n    the ''mechanism reduction'': the method is not ''persuasion trajectory detection''\n    (as Claim 2 might imply) but ''persuasion-presence detection via fine-tuned per-turn\n    embeddings.'' Reviewer R1 uses this to argue: ''The GRU architecture is unnecessary\n    complexity. The true method is: fine-tune DeBERTa on persuasion text, extract\n    [CLS] embeddings, average, classify. This is simpler than presented and the paper\n    should say so.'' This is fair criticism that the paper should preempt by reporting\n    mean pooling as the primary architecture and GRU as an (equivalent) alternative.'\n  tables:\n  - 'Table 5 (continued): Temporal Modeling Ablation. Columns: Aggregation | IID F1\n    | DD OOD F1. Rows: Bi-GRU, Mean Pooling.'\n  figures_description: []\n  analysis: 'Mean pooling equivalence is the final piece of the mechanism puzzle:\n    the method reduces to persuasion-domain fine-tuning of per-turn embeddings with\n    any reasonable aggregation. This is simultaneously a strength (simplicity, accessibility)\n    and a weakness (less novel than the full pipeline suggests). Median-tier reviewer\n    scores this as ''solid empirical contribution'' but ''not methodologically novel.'''\n","numLines":528,"startLine":1,"totalLines":528}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01L7boSGzQ5YWny2UPaaaNBx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I've now read both the paper PDF and the review context YAML. Let me summarize what I have:\n\n**Paper**: DisPeD (Discourse-level Persuasion Detection) - proposes persuasion-aware fine-tuning of DeBERTa for multi-turn jailbreak detection. Key claims: (1) cross-attack generalization via persuasion fine-tuning (+72pp over vanilla on DD), (2) interpretable strategy trajectories, (3) perturbation robustness at K=1.\n\n**Review Context**: Contains 15 imagined experiments with expected results and detailed reviewer rationale for a \"median\" tier paper. \n\nNow I'm examining the key tensions in the work: TF-IDF's surprisingly strong performance on DD, scrambled labels nearly matching proper ones, mean pooling performing as well as GRU, the model's insensitivity to lambda, concerns about synthetic OOD data validity, and the lack of human evaluation for the interpretability claims. I'm about to dispatch two reviewers in parallel—one focusing on methods and experiments, the other on novelty and related work—then run them through a four-step roundtable discussion to synthesize their perspectives.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":25881,"cache_read_input_tokens":24258,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":25881},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c762cd84-11f2-4082-a75b-ef1fc3beefd8","uuid":"f3de7195-3c7d-4a40-87aa-dc42aa820099"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01L7boSGzQ5YWny2UPaaaNBx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I have the paper and review context. Launching two parallel reviewer agents: R1 (methods + experiments) and R2 (novelty + related work)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":25881,"cache_read_input_tokens":24258,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":25881},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c762cd84-11f2-4082-a75b-ef1fc3beefd8","uuid":"3c74fb33-3915-481e-9e86-744a79f2ed4e"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01L7boSGzQ5YWny2UPaaaNBx","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01TrdrxTg4kGpaLFh5sudK76","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R1: Methods + Experiments review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **methods and experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: DisPeD: Persuasion-Aware Fine-Tuning for Robust Cross-Attack Multi-Turn Jailbreak Detection\n\n**Core idea**: Fine-tune DeBERTa-v3-base on an 8-class persuasion strategy classification task (multi-task with binary jailbreak intent), then aggregate per-turn [CLS] embeddings via mean pooling for conversation-level jailbreak detection. Trained only on Crescendo-style attacks, evaluated zero-shot on Deceptive Delight (DD) and Foot-in-the-Door (FITD).\n\n**Key claims**:\n1. Cross-attack generalization: DisPeD F1=1.0 on DD vs vanilla DeBERTa F1=0.284 (+72pp)\n2. Interpretable strategy trajectories via 8-class taxonomy\n3. Perturbation robustness at K=1: DisPeD loses 2.6pp vs TF-IDF 9.6pp vs baseline 14.6pp\n\n**Method (Section 3)**:\n- Stage 1: Multi-task fine-tuning of DeBERTa. Persuasion head: 9-class softmax (8 strategies + none). Intent head: binary sigmoid. Loss: L = L_pers + λ·L_intent. λ=0.5 default but ablation shows insensitivity (all λ∈{0,0.3,0.5,2.0} yield F1=1.0).\n- Stage 2: Extract [CLS] embeddings per turn, aggregate via mean pooling (GRU variant equivalent), linear classifier for jailbreak/benign.\n\n**Experimental Setup (Section 4)**:\n- Training: 240 topic-anchored conversation pairs (Crescendo jailbreak + matched benign), generated by Qwen3-8B\n- OOD: 80 FITD + 80 DD synthetic conversations (Qwen3-8B)\n- WildChat hard negatives: 420 high-drift benign conversations after filtering 16% jailbreak contamination\n- Baselines: Vanilla DeBERTa+GRU, TF-IDF+LR, DeepContext proxy (BERT+GRU), LLM-as-judge (GPT-5.5, Qwen3-8B)\n- Evaluation: F1 (macro), 3 seeds, bootstrap 95% CIs, K∈{1,2,3,5,all}\n\n**Key Results**:\n- Table 1: TF-IDF achieves F1=1.0 IID and FITD, 0.944 DD\n- Table 2: IID all methods saturate (DisPeD 1.0, Baseline 0.968-1.0, TF-IDF 1.0). Max gap 3.2pp at K=1\n- Table 3: DD cross-attack — DisPeD 1.0 vs Baseline 0.284 (full), but TF-IDF 0.944. FITD saturates for all methods\n- Table 4: Scrambled labels drop persuasion acc 79.6%→64.4% but detection drops only 1.3pp IID, 6.5pp OOD. Binary (none-collapse) matches 9-class within 1pp\n- Table 5: Perturbation — DisPeD 0pp drop (full), -2.6pp (K=1). Baseline -11.8pp/-14.6pp. TF-IDF -1.3pp/-9.6pp\n- Table 6: Strategy trajectories — Crescendo escalation slope 0.68, benign 0.12\n- Table 7: WildChat FPR — DisPeD 9.5%, Baseline 14.3%, TF-IDF 4.8%\n- Table 8: DeepContext proxy BERT+GRU F1=0.256 DD (vs DeBERTa baseline 0.284)\n- Table 9: GPT-5.5 F1=0.19-0.45, 30-47% unparseable; charitable correction reaches 0.7-0.8\n\n**Ablations**:\n- Lambda insensitivity: all λ values → F1=1.0\n- Mean pooling = GRU (0pp gap)\n- Checkpoint sensitivity: epoch 2-final all F1=1.0 IID, DD variance 0.011pp\n\n## Review Context (imagined experiment expectations)\nThe review context file identifies these key tensions:\n1. IID saturation: all methods converge, task is trivially separable IID\n2. Scrambled labels: the most contentious result — scrambled labels barely hurt, raising annotation artifact concerns (Gururangan et al. 2018). Without a random-task control (NLI fine-tuning on same data), cannot distinguish persuasion-specific from general domain adaptation\n3. TF-IDF achieves 0.944 on DD — practical gap to DisPeD is only 5.6pp, not 72pp\n4. Mean pooling = GRU → method reduces to \"fine-tune DeBERTa on persuasion text, average embeddings, classify\"\n5. Lambda insensitivity → multi-task framing is misleading, intent head contributes nothing\n6. FITD saturates → cross-attack advantage is attack-type-dependent\n7. All OOD data is synthetic (Qwen3-8B) — diversity/subtlety concerns\n8. No human evaluation of interpretability claim\n9. WildChat FPR 9.5% > TF-IDF 4.8%\n10. LLM-as-judge has 30-47% unparseable rate, single zero-shot prompt only\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed R1 review focusing on **methods and experimental rigor**. Structure:\n\n### 1. Summary (3-4 sentences)\n### 2. Strengths (bulleted, 4-6 points)\n### 3. Weaknesses (bulleted, 4-6 points, each with specific evidence)\n### 4. Questions for Authors (3-5 specific questions)\n### 5. Missing Experiments or Controls\n### 6. Soundness Rating (1-5, where 5=excellent)\n### 7. Overall Score (1-6 scale) with 1-sentence justification\n### 8. Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Ground criticisms in specific evidence from the paper. Consider both what the paper does well and where it falls short.","model":"opus"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":25881,"cache_read_input_tokens":24258,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":25881},"output_tokens":16,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c762cd84-11f2-4082-a75b-ef1fc3beefd8","uuid":"03e2e4fa-a523-43b1-a231-4d06ba944232"}
