5% CIs, K∈{1,2,3,5,all}\n\n**Key Results**:\n- Table 1: TF-IDF achieves F1=1.0 IID and FITD, 0.944 DD\n- Table 2: IID all methods saturate (DisPeD 1.0, Baseline 0.968-1.0, TF-IDF 1.0). Max gap 3.2pp at K=1\n- Table 3: DD cross-attack — DisPeD 1.0 vs Baseline 0.284 (full), but TF-IDF 0.944. FITD saturates for all methods\n- Table 4: Scrambled labels drop persuasion acc 79.6%→64.4% but detection drops only 1.3pp IID, 6.5pp OOD. Binary (none-collapse) matches 9-class within 1pp\n- Table 5: Perturbation — DisPeD 0pp drop (full), -2.6pp (K=1). Baseline -11.8pp/-14.6pp. TF-IDF -1.3pp/-9.6pp\n- Table 6: Strategy trajectories — Crescendo escalation slope 0.68, benign 0.12\n- Table 7: WildChat FPR — DisPeD 9.5%, Baseline 14.3%, TF-IDF 4.8%\n- Table 8: DeepContext proxy BERT+GRU F1=0.256 DD (vs DeBERTa baseline 0.284)\n- Table 9: GPT-5.5 F1=0.19-0.45, 30-47% unparseable; charitable correction reaches 0.7-0.8\n\n**Ablations**:\n- Lambda insensitivity: all λ values → F1=1.0\n- Mean pooling = GRU (0pp gap)\n- Checkpoint sensitivity: epoch 2-final all F1=1.0 IID, DD variance 0.011pp\n\n## Review Context (imagined experiment expectations)\nThe review context file identifies these key tensions:\n1. IID saturation: all methods converge, task is trivially separable IID\n2. Scrambled labels: the most contentious result — scrambled labels barely hurt, raising annotation artifact concerns (Gururangan et al. 2018). Without a random-task control (NLI fine-tuning on same data), cannot distinguish persuasion-specific from general domain adaptation\n3. TF-IDF achieves 0.944 on DD — practical gap to DisPeD is only 5.6pp, not 72pp\n4. Mean pooling = GRU → method reduces to \"fine-tune DeBERTa on persuasion text, average embeddings, classify\"\n5. Lambda insensitivity → multi-task framing is misleading, intent head contributes nothing\n6. FITD saturates → cross-attack advantage is attack-type-dependent\n7. All OOD data is synthetic (Qwen3-8B) — diversity/subtlety concerns\n8. No human evaluation of interpretability claim\n9. WildChat FPR 9.5% > TF-IDF 4.8%\n10. LLM-as-judge has 30-47% unparseable rate, single zero-shot prompt only\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed R1 review focusing on **methods and experimental rigor**. Structure:\n\n### 1. Summary (3-4 sentences)\n### 2. Strengths (bulleted, 4-6 points)\n### 3. Weaknesses (bulleted, 4-6 points, each with specific evidence)\n### 4. Questions for Authors (3-5 specific questions)\n### 5. Missing Experiments or Controls\n### 6. Soundness Rating (1-5, where 5=excellent)\n### 7. Overall Score (1-6 scale) with 1-sentence justification\n### 8. Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Ground criticisms in specific evidence from the paper. Consider both what the paper does well and where it falls short.","uuid":"1a29f6a0-6da6-47d9-aaaa-ceb8cb0cbc73","session_id":"c762cd84-11f2-4082-a75b-ef1fc3beefd8"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R1 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 6=Strong Accept). Your focus is **methods and experiments**.\n\n## Paper Summary\n\n**Title**: DisPeD: Persuasion-Aware Fine-Tuning for Robust Cross-Attack Multi-Turn Jailbreak Detection\n\n**Core idea**: Fine-tune DeBERTa-v3-base on an 8-class persuasion strategy classification task (multi-task with binary jailbreak intent), then aggregate per-turn [CLS] embeddings via mean pooling for conversation-level jailbreak detection. Trained only on Crescendo-style attacks, evaluated zero-shot on Deceptive Delight (DD) and Foot-in-the-Door (FITD).\n\n**Key claims**:\n1. Cross-attack generalization: DisPeD F1=1.0 on DD vs vanilla DeBERTa F1=0.284 (+72pp)\n2. Interpretable strategy trajectories via 8-class taxonomy\n3. Perturbation robustness at K=1: DisPeD loses 2.6pp vs TF-IDF 9.6pp vs baseline 14.6pp\n\n**Method (Section 3)**:\n- Stage 1: Multi-task fine-tuning of DeBERTa. Persuasion head: 9-class softmax (8 strategies + none). Intent head: binary sigmoid. Loss: L = L_pers + λ·L_intent. λ=0.5 default but ablation shows insensitivity (all λ∈{0,0.3,0.5,2.0} yield F1=1.0).\n- Stage 2: Extract [CLS] embeddings per turn, aggregate via mean pooling (GRU variant equivalent), linear classifier for jailbreak/benign.\n\n**Experimental Setup (Section 4)**:\n- Training: 240 topic-anchored conversation pairs (Crescendo jailbreak + matched benign), generated by Qwen3-8B\n- OOD: 80 FITD + 80 DD synthetic conversations (Qwen3-8B)\n- WildChat hard negatives: 420 high-drift benign conversations after filtering 16% jailbreak contamination\n- Baselines: Vanilla DeBERTa+GRU, TF-IDF+LR, DeepContext proxy (BERT+GRU), LLM-as-judge (GPT-5.5, Qwen3-8B)\n- Evaluation: F1 (macro), 3 seeds, bootstrap 95% CIs, K∈{1,2,3,5,all}\n\n**Key Results**:\n- Table 1: TF-IDF achieves F1=1.0 IID and FITD, 0.944 DD\n- Table 2: IID all methods saturate (DisPeD 1.0, Baseline 0.968-1.0, TF-IDF 1.0). Max gap 3.2pp at K=1\n- Table 3: DD cross-attack — DisPeD 1.0 vs Baseline 0.284 (full), but TF-IDF 0.944. FITD saturates for all methods\n- Table 4: Scrambled labels drop persuasion acc 79.6%→64.4% but detection drops only 1.3pp IID, 6.5pp OOD. Binary (none-collapse) matches 9-class within 1pp\n- Table 5: Perturbation — DisPeD 0pp drop (full), -2.6pp (K=1). Baseline -11.8pp/-14.6pp. TF-IDF -1.3pp/-9.6pp\n- Table 6: Strategy trajectories — Crescendo escalation slope 0.68, benign 0.12\n- Table 7: WildChat FPR — DisPeD 9.5%, Baseline 14.3%, TF-IDF 4.8%\n- Table 8: DeepContext proxy BERT+GRU F1=0.256 DD (vs DeBERTa baseline 0.284)\n- Table 9: GPT-5.5 F1=0.19-0.45, 30-47% unparseable; charitable correction reaches 0.7-0.8\n\n**Ablations**:\n- Lambda insensitivity: all λ values → F1=1.0\n- Mean pooling = GRU (0pp gap)\n- Checkpoint sensitivity: epoch 2-final all F1=1.0 IID, DD variance 0.011pp\n\n## Review Context (imagined experiment expectations)\nThe review context file identifies these key tensions:\n1. IID saturation: all methods converge, task is trivially separable IID\n2. Scrambled labels: the most contentious result — scrambled labels barely hurt, raising annotation artifact concerns (Gururangan et al. 2018). Without a random-task control (NLI fine-tuning on same data), cannot distinguish persuasion-specific from general domain adaptation\n3. TF-IDF achieves 0.944 on DD — practical gap to DisPeD is only 5.6pp, not 72pp\n4. Mean pooling = GRU → method reduces to \"fine-tune DeBERTa on persuasion text, average embeddings, classify\"\n5. Lambda insensitivity → multi-task framing is misleading, intent head contributes nothing\n6. FITD saturates → cross-attack advantage is attack-type-dependent\n7. All OOD data is synthetic (Qwen3-8B) — diversity/subtlety concerns\n8. No human evaluation of interpretability claim\n9. WildChat FPR 9.5% > TF-IDF 4.8%\n10. LLM-as-judge has 30-47% unparseable rate, single zero-shot prompt only\n\n## Your Task\n\nWrite a detailed R1 review focusing on **methods and experimental rigor**. Structure:\n\n### 1. Summary (3-4 sentences)\n### 2. Strengths (bulleted, 4-6 points)\n### 3. Weaknesses (bulleted, 4-6 points, each with specific evidence)\n### 4. Questions for Authors (3-5 specific questions)\n### 5. Missing Experiments or Controls\n### 6. Soundness Rating (1-5, where 5=excellent)\n### 7. Overall Score (1-6 scale) with 1-sentence justification\n### 8. Confidence (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Ground criticisms in specific evidence from the paper. Consider both what the paper does well and where it falls short."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01TrdrxTg4kGpaLFh5sudK76","session_id":"c762cd84-11f2-4082-a75b-ef1fc3beefd8","uuid":"74d5e188-555e-40de-a0b7-9cec0e0e8182","timestamp":"2026-05-18T04:03:23.787Z"}
