{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ALv9zSZfUCRH8rSzPnvbZ9","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## R1 Response to R2's Review\n\n**1. Strongest agreement**\n\nR2's \"novelty paradox\" framing (point 1) articulates precisely what I flagged as W5 but more sharply. The ablation suite is indeed the paper's most rigorous contribution, yet it systematically dismantles the multi-task architecture the paper proposes. I also strongly endorse R2's TF-IDF analysis (point 2): the 0.944 F1 on DD makes the headline +72pp improvement misleading, and R2 is right to note that DisPeD's FPR is actually worse than TF-IDF (9.5% vs 4.8%). This detail I underweighted in my own review.\n\n**2. Disagreements or different emphasis**\n\nR2's related work critique (point 3) is valid but I consider it a correctable weakness, not a structural flaw. Missing citations can be added in revision; the scrambled-label confound and synthetic-only evaluation cannot. I would deprioritize the related work gaps relative to the experimental design issues.\n\nOn the \"Pragmatic Evasion Asymmetry\" critique (point 6), I agree it is unsupported, but I see it more as speculative framing in the discussion than a core claim the paper's validity depends on. R2 may be treating it as more central than the authors intended.\n\nI also think R2's characterization of the method as pure \"domain-adaptive fine-tuning\" is slightly too reductive. The persuasion-strategy classification head does produce interpretable per-strategy scores, which is a genuine functional difference from vanilla continued pretraining, even if the ablations show the classification loss contributes minimally to detection accuracy. The interpretability angle has real value; the paper just fails to evaluate it rigorously.\n\n**3. Score change**\n\nR2's review does not change my score. We converge on the same fundamental problems (synthetic-only evaluation, scrambled-label confound, TF-IDF baseline) from different angles, which reinforces rather than revises my assessment. I remain at **3/6 (Weak Reject)**.\n\n**4. Single most important issue for the meta-review**\n\nThe scrambled-label confound. Both R1 and R2 independently identify this as critical and unresolved. Without a domain-adaptation control (MLM-only continued pretraining on the same persuasion text, or an alternative-task control like NLI), the paper cannot support its central claim that persuasion-strategy supervision drives detection performance. If the gains come from exposure to persuasion-domain text rather than the classification objective, the entire theoretical motivation collapses. This is the one issue that, if addressed, would most change both reviewers' assessments, and if left unaddressed, is sufficient grounds for rejection on its own."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: aa4ce210cc581f93b (use SendMessage with to: 'aa4ce210cc581f93b' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 16707\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 19622</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c762cd84-11f2-4082-a75b-ef1fc3beefd8","uuid":"7efa57e0-2296-415a-9a22-9eec74ffee2f","timestamp":"2026-05-18T04:07:28.673Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R1 (methods + experiments focus). You previously gave this paper a 3/6 (Weak Reject). Now read R2's review below and provide a brief response (under 400 words):\n\n1. Which of R2's points do you agree with most strongly?\n2. Which do you disagree with or see differently?\n3. Does R2's review change your score? If so, in which direction and why?\n4. What is the single most important issue the meta-review should highlight?\n\n## R2's Review (Novelty + Related Work)\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject), Novelty: 2/5, Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**Key points from R2:**\n\n1. **Novelty paradox**: The ablation suite (the paper's greatest intellectual contribution) simultaneously undermines the technical contribution. Every component beyond \"fine-tune on persuasion text\" is shown unnecessary. Method reduces to domain-adaptive fine-tuning.\n\n2. **TF-IDF elephant**: TF-IDF=0.944 on DD means the +72pp headline is computed against an anomalously bad vanilla baseline. TF-IDF closes 93% of the gap without neural components. DisPeD's practical advantage over TF-IDF: (a) 7pp at K=1 under perturbation, (b) interpretability. But DisPeD has higher FPR (9.5% vs 4.8%).\n\n3. **Related work gaps**: Missing ActorAttack/SafeMTData, Li et al. 2024 (\"LLM Defenses Are Not Robust\"), Cialdini 2001, placeholder citations for BIID/SafeDream, insufficient positioning against Jaipersaud et al. 2025.\n\n4. **Scrambled-label confound**: Without MLM-only continued pretraining control or alternative-task control (NLI on same text), cannot distinguish persuasion supervision from domain adaptation.\n\n5. **Synthetic-only evaluation on n=80**: Cross-attack claim rests on a single synthetic DD dataset. FITD saturates.\n\n6. **Pragmatic Evasion Asymmetry unsupported**: Paper claims pragmatic features harder to evade but provides no evidence.\n\nR2 concludes the paper is an \"incremental operationalization of known observations using standard techniques, evaluated on small synthetic datasets.\"\n\n## Your previous R1 review key points for reference:\n- W1: All evaluation data synthetic from same pipeline (Critical)\n- W2: Missing domain-adaptation control for scrambled-label confound (Critical)\n- W3: Ceiling effects from small test sets (Major)\n- W4: TF-IDF narrows practical contribution (Major)\n- W5: Multi-task framing misleading given ablations (Major)\n- W6: FITD saturation eliminates half cross-attack evaluation (Minor-Major)\n- Score: 3/6, Soundness: 2.5/5, Confidence: 4/5","agentId":"aa4ce210cc581f93b","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## R1 Response to R2's Review\n\n**1. Strongest agreement**\n\nR2's \"novelty paradox\" framing (point 1) articulates precisely what I flagged as W5 but more sharply. The ablation suite is indeed the paper's most rigorous contribution, yet it systematically dismantles the multi-task architecture the paper proposes. I also strongly endorse R2's TF-IDF analysis (point 2): the 0.944 F1 on DD makes the headline +72pp improvement misleading, and R2 is right to note that DisPeD's FPR is actually worse than TF-IDF (9.5% vs 4.8%). This detail I underweighted in my own review.\n\n**2. Disagreements or different emphasis**\n\nR2's related work critique (point 3) is valid but I consider it a correctable weakness, not a structural flaw. Missing citations can be added in revision; the scrambled-label confound and synthetic-only evaluation cannot. I would deprioritize the related work gaps relative to the experimental design issues.\n\nOn the \"Pragmatic Evasion Asymmetry\" critique (point 6), I agree it is unsupported, but I see it more as speculative framing in the discussion than a core claim the paper's validity depends on. R2 may be treating it as more central than the authors intended.\n\nI also think R2's characterization of the method as pure \"domain-adaptive fine-tuning\" is slightly too reductive. The persuasion-strategy classification head does produce interpretable per-strategy scores, which is a genuine functional difference from vanilla continued pretraining, even if the ablations show the classification loss contributes minimally to detection accuracy. The interpretability angle has real value; the paper just fails to evaluate it rigorously.\n\n**3. Score change**\n\nR2's review does not change my score. We converge on the same fundamental problems (synthetic-only evaluation, scrambled-label confound, TF-IDF baseline) from different angles, which reinforces rather than revises my assessment. I remain at **3/6 (Weak Reject)**.\n\n**4. Single most important issue for the meta-review**\n\nThe scrambled-label confound. Both R1 and R2 independently identify this as critical and unresolved. Without a domain-adaptation control (MLM-only continued pretraining on the same persuasion text, or an alternative-task control like NLI), the paper cannot support its central claim that persuasion-strategy supervision drives detection performance. If the gains come from exposure to persuasion-domain text rather than the classification objective, the entire theoretical motivation collapses. This is the one issue that, if addressed, would most change both reviewers' assessments, and if left unaddressed, is sufficient grounds for rejection on its own."}],"totalDurationMs":19622,"totalTokens":16707,"totalToolUseCount":0,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7802,"cache_read_input_tokens":8255,"output_tokens":647,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7802},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":647,"cache_read_input_tokens":8255,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7802,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7802,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}}}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_notification","task_id":"a1c2e0e45407c521c","tool_use_id":"toolu_01DL2t4NUp58LzB2xK7exNBv","status":"completed","output_file":"","summary":"R2 cross-review response","usage":{"total_tokens":16142,"tool_uses":0,"duration_ms":21111},"uuid":"c8a4dce9-efbe-47c6-8c8d-535029247863","session_id":"c762cd84-11f2-4082-a75b-ef1fc3beefd8"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01DL2t4NUp58LzB2xK7exNBv","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R2 Response to R1's Review\n\n**1. Strongest agreement**\n\nR1's W2 (missing domain-adaptive pretraining control) is the point I agree with most strongly, and it reinforces my own core concern from a different angle. I framed this as the \"novelty paradox\" — ablations show the persuasion-specific supervision adds little beyond what simpler methods achieve. R1 sharpens this into a concrete experimental demand: without an MLM-only continued pretraining baseline on the same persuasion text, we cannot determine whether the gains come from persuasion-aware supervision or mere domain exposure. This is exactly the kind of confound that, once articulated, cannot be set aside. The missing Gururangan et al. (2020) citation is also a significant related work gap I should have flagged myself.\n\n**2. Disagreements or different readings**\n\nI read W5 (multi-task framing is misleading) as minor rather than major. The two-stage description — fine-tune then embed-and-classify — is architecturally accurate even if conceptually simple. Calling it \"misleading\" overstates the issue; \"overclaimed\" is more precise. The method section could be streamlined, but I would not treat presentation style as a methodological weakness on par with the missing controls.\n\nI also weight W4 differently. R1 frames TF-IDF's F1=0.944 as narrowing the practical contribution to a statistically indistinguishable 5.6pp gap. I agree on the statistics, but I think the deeper problem is conceptual: if a bag-of-words baseline reaches 0.944, the task itself may be too easy in its current formulation, which is a more fundamental concern than the delta size.\n\n**3. Score change**\n\nR1's review does not change my score. It strengthens the case for Weak Reject by adding the domain-adaptive pretraining confound (W2) and the ceiling-effect statistical argument (W3), both of which I find compelling. But my score was already 3/6 based on the novelty paradox and TF-IDF near-parity, which are closely related concerns. If anything, R1's analysis makes me more confident in that score rather than moving it in either direction.\n\n**4. Single most important issue for the meta-review**\n\nThe meta-review should highlight the **inability to distinguish persuasion-specific learning from domain exposure**. This subsumes multiple weaknesses: R1's W2 (no pretraining control), my novelty paradox (ablations erode the contribution), and the TF-IDF near-parity (a lexical baseline nearly matches). Together these show that the paper's central claim — that modeling persuasion structure improves jailbreak detection — is not supported by the current experimental design. Until a domain-adaptive pretraining control is run and the method still shows clear gains, the technical contribution remains unestablished."},{"type":"text","text":"agentId: a1c2e0e45407c521c (use SendMessage with to: 'a1c2e0e45407c521c' to continue this agent)\n<usage>total_tokens: 16806\ntool_uses: 0\nduration_ms: 21113</usage>"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c762cd84-11f2-4082-a75b-ef1fc3beefd8","uuid":"d846b045-56e9-4f65-967f-a29f7739b166","timestamp":"2026-05-18T04:07:45.499Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 (novelty + related work focus). You previously gave this paper a 3/6 (Weak Reject). Now read R1's review below and provide a brief response (under 400 words):\n\n1. Which of R1's points do you agree with most strongly?\n2. Which do you disagree with or see differently?\n3. Does R1's review change your score? If so, in which direction and why?\n4. What is the single most important issue the meta-review should highlight?\n\n## R1's Review (Methods + Experiments)\n\n**Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject), Soundness: 2.5/5, Confidence: 4/5**\n\n**Key strengths R1 identified:**\n- S1: Large +72pp headline delta, baseline's monotonic degradation on DD is genuinely novel observation\n- S2: Unusually thorough and self-aware ablation\n- S3: Honest TF-IDF positioning\n- S4: Well-designed perturbation experiment\n- S5: Useful LLM-as-judge comparison\n- S6: Reproducibility-friendly simplicity\n\n**Key weaknesses R1 identified:**\n\n- W1 (Critical): All evaluation data synthetic from same Qwen3-8B pipeline. Training, IID test, and OOD test may share generation-pipeline artifacts. Without real-world attack data, ecological validity is unestablished.\n\n- W2 (Critical): Missing domain-adaptive pretraining control. Without MLM-only continued pretraining or alternative-task control on same text, cannot distinguish persuasion supervision from domain exposure. Paper also misses citing Gururangan et al. (ACL 2020) \"Don't Stop Pretraining.\"\n\n- W3 (Major): Perfect F1=1.0 on n=72 IID and n=80 OOD creates irresolvable ceiling effects. By rule of three, 95% upper bound on error rate is 7.2%, so true F1 could be as low as 0.93. Cannot meaningfully discriminate between method variants.\n\n- W4 (Major): TF-IDF at F1=0.944 narrows practical contribution to 5.6pp, which is not statistically distinguishable from zero on n=80.\n\n- W5 (Major): Multi-task framing is misleading. Actual method is \"fine-tune on persuasion text, average embeddings, classify\" but paper describes complex two-stage architecture.\n\n- W6 (Minor-Major): FITD saturation leaves DD as the sole discriminating attack type.\n\n**R1's missing experiments:**\n1. Domain-adaptive pretraining control (Critical)\n2. Alternative auxiliary task control (Major)\n3. Real-world multi-turn attack evaluation (Critical)\n4. PR curves on WildChat hard negatives\n5. Additional OOD attack types\n6. Exact counts and confidence intervals\n\n## Your previous R2 review key points for reference:\n- Novelty paradox: ablations undermine technical contribution\n- TF-IDF closes 93% of gap\n- Related work gaps (ActorAttack, Li et al., Cialdini, placeholder citations)\n- Scrambled-label confound without proper controls\n- Score: 3/6, Novelty: 2/5, Confidence: 4/5","agentId":"a1c2e0e45407c521c","agentType":"general-purpose","content":[{"type":"text","text":"\n\n## R2 Response to R1's Review\n\n**1. Strongest agreement**\n\nR1's W2 (missing domain-adaptive pretraining control) is the point I agree with most strongly, and it reinforces my own core concern from a different angle. I framed this as the \"novelty paradox\" — ablations show the persuasion-specific supervision adds little beyond what simpler methods achieve. R1 sharpens this into a concrete experimental demand: without an MLM-only continued pretraining baseline on the same persuasion text, we cannot determine whether the gains come from persuasion-aware supervision or mere domain exposure. This is exactly the kind of confound that, once articulated, cannot be set aside. The missing Gururangan et al. (2020) citation is also a significant related work gap I should have flagged myself.\n\n**2. Disagreements or different readings**\n\nI read W5 (multi-task framing is misleading) as minor rather than major. The two-stage description — fine-tune then embed-and-classify — is architecturally accurate even if conceptually simple. Calling it \"misleading\" overstates the issue; \"overclaimed\" is more precise. The method section could be streamlined, but I would not treat presentation style as a methodological weakness on par with the missing controls.\n\nI also weight W4 differently. R1 frames TF-IDF's F1=0.944 as narrowing the practical contribution to a statistically indistinguishable 5.6pp gap. I agree on the statistics, but I think the deeper problem is conceptual: if a bag-of-words baseline reaches 0.944, the task itself may be too easy in its current formulation, which is a more fundamental concern than the delta size.\n\n**3. Score change**\n\nR1's review does not change my score. It strengthens the case for Weak Reject by adding the domain-adaptive pretraining confound (W2) and the ceiling-effect statistical argument (W3), both of which I find compelling. But my score was already 3/6 based on the novelty paradox and TF-IDF near-parity, which are closely related concerns. If anything, R1's analysis makes me more confident in that score rather than moving it in either direction.\n\n**4. Single most important issue for the meta-review**\n\nThe meta-review should highlight the **inability to distinguish persuasion-specific learning from domain exposure**. This subsumes multiple weaknesses: R1's W2 (no pretraining control), my novelty paradox (ablations erode the contribution), and the TF-IDF near-parity (a lexical baseline nearly matches). Together these show that the paper's central claim — that modeling persuasion structure improves jailbreak detection — is not supported by the current experimental design. Until a domain-adaptive pretraining control is run and the method still shows clear gains, the technical contribution remains unestablished."}],"totalDurationMs":21113,"totalTokens":16806,"totalToolUseCount":0,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7882,"cache_read_input_tokens":8255,"output_tokens":666,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7882},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":666,"cache_read_input_tokens":8255,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7882,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7882,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0194JBLGBS5F25WXhFKHfXZ4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Step 2 (cross-sharing) is complete. Both reviewers maintain 3/6 and converge on the same core issues. Now I need to do Step 3 (convergence) and Step 4 (synthesis) — I'll synthesize the final consolidated review report myself.\n\nKey convergence points:\n1. Both agree: 3/6 (Weak Reject)\n2. Both agree the #1 issue is the inability to distinguish persuasion-specific learning from domain exposure (missing domain-adaptive pretraining control)\n\n\n3. Both flag synthetic-only evaluation as a critical limitation\n4. Both see the 0.944 TF-IDF baseline as substantially narrowing the contribution\n5. Both acknowledge the ablations are thorough but actually weaken the novelty claims\n6. Both appreciate the authors' honest self-assessment\n\nThe main tension is how to interpret the multi-task framing — R1 views it as misleading while R2 sees it as overclaimed but not deceptive. They also diverge slightly on whether the related work gaps are fundamental or fixable, and whether TF-IDF signals an easy task or just a narrow practical window. Let me synthesize these into the final report.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3385,"cache_read_input_tokens":61488,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3385},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c762cd84-11f2-4082-a75b-ef1fc3beefd8","uuid":"57333ec4-1aa7-40b3-ba9b-9dde6e051cb9"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0194JBLGBS5F25WXhFKHfXZ4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Step 2 complete. Both reviewers maintain 3/6 and converge strongly on the core issue: inability to distinguish persuasion-specific learning from domain exposure. Now synthesizing the final report (Steps 3-4)."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3385,"cache_read_input_tokens":61488,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3385},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c762cd84-11f2-4082-a75b-ef1fc3beefd8","uuid":"8591f081-0723-4898-ab50-049741ea0a56"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_0194JBLGBS5F25WXhFKHfXZ4","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01RAwuA1kPp8wYmnMDJHaawH","name":"ToolSearch","input":{"query":"select:mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","max_results":1},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3385,"cache_read_input_tokens":61488,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3385},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c762cd84-11f2-4082-a75b-ef1fc3beefd8","uuid":"7baefef7-1d15-4371-977c-512e42d13d0e"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"tool_result","tool_use_id":"toolu_01RAwuA1kPp8wYmnMDJHaawH","content":[{"type":"tool_reference","tool_name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c762cd84-11f2-4082-a75b-ef1fc3beefd8","uuid":"73b0eef5-9754-49f8-ad17-87b573bc1c1b","timestamp":"2026-05-18T04:07:57.379Z","tool_use_result":{"matches":["mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report"],"query":"select:mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","total_deferred_tools":23}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01RC1nSoC9gytU21zGi2Qp8F","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_019SAH68JeV1fm83Mcf9PWvo","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report: DisPeD\n\n**Paper**: DisPeD: Persuasion-Aware Fine-Tuning for Robust Cross-Attack Multi-Turn Jailbreak Detection\n\n**Review Mode**: Full (2 Reviewers, complete roundtable)\n\n---\n\n## Consolidated Assessment\n\n**Overall Score: 3/6 (Weak Reject)**\n\nBoth reviewers independently scored 3/6 (Weak Reject) and maintained their scores after cross-review. Convergence is strong: the core concerns are shared and mutually reinforcing.\n\n---\n\n## Paper Summary\n\nDisPeD fine-tunes DeBERTa-v3-base on an 8-class persuasion strategy classification task (multi-task with binary jailbreak intent), then aggregates per-turn [CLS] embeddings via mean pooling for conversation-level jailbreak detection. Trained exclusively on 240 synthetic Crescendo attack/benign pairs (Qwen3-8B), the system achieves F1=1.0 on unseen Deceptive Delight (DD) attacks where a vanilla DeBERTa+GRU baseline collapses to F1=0.284. Comprehensive ablations reveal that the sole critical ingredient is fine-tuning DeBERTa on persuasion-related text; label granularity (binary vs 9-class: 1pp gap), label correctness (scrambled vs proper: 1-6pp gap), temporal architecture (mean pooling vs GRU: 0pp gap), and multi-task weighting (all lambda values: 0pp gap) are all interchangeable. A TF-IDF+LR baseline achieves F1=0.944 on DD without any neural components.\n\n---\n\n## Reviewer Scores\n\n| Dimension | R1 (Methods+Experiments) | R2 (Novelty+Related Work) |\n|-----------|--------------------------|---------------------------|\n| Overall | 3/6 (Weak Reject) | 3/6 (Weak Reject) |\n| Soundness | 2.5/5 | — |\n| Novelty | — | 2/5 |\n| Confidence | 4/5 | 4/5 |\n\n---\n\n## Consensus Strengths\n\n**S1. Exceptionally honest and thorough ablation suite.** The paper tests four independent ablation axes (label quality, label granularity, loss weighting, temporal architecture) and honestly reports that the method reduces to domain-specific fine-tuning. Both reviewers called this \"rare,\" \"commendable,\" and the paper's strongest intellectual contribution. The scrambled-label finding (F1=0.935-0.990 OOD despite randomized labels) is genuinely informative about how domain-adaptive fine-tuning works.\n\n**S2. Transparent positioning against TF-IDF.** Rather than hiding the strong lexical baseline (F1=0.944 on DD), the paper reports it prominently and narrows its claims accordingly. Both reviewers noted this intellectual honesty strengthens credibility, even as it constrains the claim space.\n\n**S3. Large, real headline delta on DD.** The +72pp gap between treatment and vanilla DeBERTa baseline on DD is dramatic and difficult to dismiss. The vanilla baseline's monotonic degradation with conversation length (K=1: 0.575 to full: 0.284) is itself an interesting observation about how DD's interleaving structure actively misleads generic embeddings.\n\n**S4. Perturbation robustness at K=1.** The 7pp gap over TF-IDF under adversarial paraphrasing at early detection (DisPeD: -2.6pp vs TF-IDF: -9.6pp vs baseline: -14.6pp) is a concrete, deployable advantage and the strongest differentiation point from TF-IDF.\n\n**S5. LLM-as-judge comparison.** Showing GPT-5.5 achieves at most F1=0.45 (with 30-47% unparseable rate) provides useful practical context and strengthens the case for specialized detectors over content-level reasoning approaches.\n\n---\n\n## Consensus Weaknesses\n\n### Critical Issues\n\n**W1. Cannot distinguish persuasion-specific learning from domain exposure (Critical — both reviewers' #1 concern).**\nThe scrambled-label ablation is the paper's most contentious result. Scrambled labels reduce persuasion classification accuracy from 79.6% to 64.4% but downstream detection drops only 1.3pp IID and 6.5pp OOD. This raises a fundamental confound: is the encoder learning from persuasion strategy supervision (the paper's claim), or merely from exposure to persuasion-domain text during fine-tuning? Two missing controls are needed to resolve this:\n- (a) Continued pretraining (MLM objective, no classification labels) on the same persuasion-annotated text corpus\n- (b) Fine-tuning on an unrelated auxiliary task (sentiment, NLI) using the same text\n\nWithout these, the paper cannot support its central claim. Both reviewers independently identified this as the single issue that, if addressed, would most change their assessment. The paper also does not cite or engage with Gururangan et al. (ACL 2020, \"Don't Stop Pretraining\"), the canonical work on domain-adaptive pretraining.\n\n**W2. All evaluation data is synthetic from the same pipeline (Critical).**\nTraining data (480 conversations), IID test data (n=72), and OOD test data (80 FITD + 80 DD) are all generated by Qwen3-8B through the authors' pipeline. Cross-attack \"generalization\" may partly reflect shared generation-pipeline artifacts (vocabulary register, sentence structure, conversational formality) rather than genuine robustness to novel attack types. Without evaluation on at least one source of real multi-turn attacks (ActorAttack/SafeMTData, real Crescendo logs, Li et al. 2024 red-teaming data), ecological validity is unestablished.\n\n### Major Issues\n\n**W3. TF-IDF at F1=0.944 substantially narrows the practical contribution.**\nThe headline +72pp advantage is computed against a vanilla DeBERTa baseline that is anomalously bad on DD (F1=0.284). Against TF-IDF, the practical margin is 5.6pp on DD at full conversation length. On n=80 (40 positive DD samples), this gap is not statistically distinguishable from zero. R2 framed this more fundamentally: if a bag-of-words baseline reaches 0.944, the task may be too easy in its current formulation. DisPeD also has higher FPR than TF-IDF on WildChat hard negatives (9.5% vs 4.8%).\n\n**W4. The \"novelty paradox\" — ablations undermine the technical contribution.**\nThe paper presents a two-stage architecture with 8-class taxonomy, GRU aggregation, and multi-task loss, then systematically shows none of these components matter. What survives is \"fine-tune DeBERTa on persuasion-related text, average [CLS] embeddings, classify.\" This is a standard transfer learning recipe. The ablation suite is the paper's greatest intellectual contribution, but it simultaneously reduces the method's novelty to an application insight (persuasion-domain fine-tuning transfers across attack types), not a methodological advance.\n\n**W5. Ceiling effects from small test sets prevent meaningful comparison.**\nF1=1.0 with zero variance across 3 seeds on n=72 IID and n=80 OOD. By the rule of three, the 95% upper bound on error rate is 7.2%, meaning true F1 could be as low as 0.93. The pervasive ceiling prevents distinguishing treatment, binary-collapse, scrambled-label, and different-lambda variants (all 0.99-1.0). The paper cannot distinguish \"the method works perfectly\" from \"the test set is insufficiently challenging.\"\n\n**W6. FITD saturation eliminates half the cross-attack evaluation.**\nOn FITD, all methods (treatment, baseline, TF-IDF) achieve F1 approximately 1.0. Cross-attack advantage materializes on exactly one OOD attack type (DD). With only one discriminating evaluation dataset, the generalization claim is fragile.\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n**W7. Related work gaps.** Missing citations: ActorAttack/SafeMTData (Ren et al. 2024), Li et al. 2024 (\"LLM Defenses Are Not Robust\"), Cialdini 2001 (foundational persuasion theory), Gururangan et al. 2020 (\"Don't Stop Pretraining\"). Placeholder citations for BIID and SafeDream (no proper bibliographic entries). Insufficient positioning against Jaipersaud/Xiao et al. 2025 (linear probes already extract persuasion dynamics from representations).\n\n**W8. No human evaluation of interpretability claim.** Strategy trajectory analysis (Claim 2) lacks any user study or expert evaluation confirming the trajectories are interpretable or actionable in practice. Given that the ablations show the taxonomy is unnecessary for detection, the interpretability claim is the taxonomy's sole justification and needs empirical support.\n\n**W9. LLM-as-judge baseline uses single zero-shot prompt only.** 30-47% unparseable rate undermines the comparison. Chain-of-thought, few-shot, or conversation-level analysis prompting was not tested. Under charitable correction (all unparseable assumed correct), GPT-5.5 could reach F1 approximately 0.7-0.8, narrowing the advantage.\n\n---\n\n## Key Questions for Authors\n\n1. **The critical missing control:** Would continued pretraining (MLM objective, no classification head) on the same persuasion text corpus bridge the OOD gap? This single experiment would determine whether persuasion supervision or domain exposure drives the gains.\n\n2. **Synthetic data confound:** Were DD and FITD test conversations generated by the same Qwen3-8B model and annotation pipeline as training data? What prevents shared generation artifacts from inflating cross-attack transfer?\n\n3. **Statistical significance:** Can you provide exact TP/FP/FN/TN counts and exact binomial confidence intervals for Tables 2-5? Is the 5.6pp DisPeD-TF-IDF gap on DD (n=80) statistically significant?\n\n4. **Topic anchoring in OOD:** Were benign conversations in the OOD test sets topic-anchored to OOD attack topics? Could the DD result partly reflect topic divergence detectable by any encoder?\n\n5. **Per-turn safety classifiers:** Why were Llama Guard and WildGuard not evaluated as baselines? Running them on concatenated first-K turns would be informative.\n\n---\n\n## Missing Experiments (Priority-Ordered)\n\n1. **Domain-adaptive pretraining control** — MLM-only continued pretraining on same persuasion text (Critical)\n2. **Real-world multi-turn attack evaluation** — even 30-50 real examples would transform ecological validity (Critical)\n3. **Alternative auxiliary task control** — NLI or sentiment fine-tuning on same text to isolate task contribution (Major)\n4. **Additional OOD attack types** — at least one more structurally distinct attack family beyond DD (Major)\n5. **Precision-recall curves** — full PR curves on WildChat hard negatives across thresholds (Minor)\n6. **Human evaluation of interpretability** — domain expert assessment of strategy trajectory actionability (Minor)\n\n---\n\n## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nComparing against the review context (imagined experiment list):\n\n| Experiment | Status | Notes |\n|-----------|--------|-------|\n| core_revalidation_clean | Covered (Table 2) | IID ceiling confirmed |\n| scrambled_label_ablation | Covered (Table 4) | Key confound identified |\n| adversarial_perturbation | Covered (Table 5) | Strongest TF-IDF differentiator at K=1 |\n| multi_attack_generation | Covered (Section 4.1) | Synthetic data quality concern flagged |\n| cross_attack_evaluation | Covered (Table 3) | Core result, DD-only advantage noted |\n| tfidf_lr_baseline | Covered (Table 1) | Honestly presented |\n| strategy_transition_analysis | Covered (Table 6, Section 5.5) | No human evaluation |\n| wildchat_hard_negatives | Covered (Table 7) | FPR worse than TF-IDF |\n| cross_checkpoint_variance | Covered (Section 5.3) | Clean positive result |\n| deepcontext_proxy | Covered (Table 8) | Proxy, not reproduction |\n| llm_as_judge_baseline | Covered (Table 9) | Single prompt limitation noted |\n| none_collapse_ablation | Covered (Table 4) | Binary matches 9-class |\n| lambda_ablation | Covered (Section 5.3) | Complete insensitivity |\n| mean_pooling_baseline | Covered (Section 5.3) | GRU unnecessary |\n\nCoverage is comprehensive. The paper includes all imagined experiments. The primary gaps are missing controls (domain-adaptive pretraining, alternative tasks) and missing real-world data, not missing planned experiments.\n\n---\n\n## Rebuttal Guidance\n\n**What could change the score to borderline accept (4/6):**\n- Present MLM-only continued pretraining results showing it does NOT bridge the OOD gap (proving persuasion supervision matters beyond domain exposure)\n- Add evaluation on at least one real-world multi-turn attack dataset\n- Provide statistical significance tests for the DisPeD-TF-IDF gap on DD\n\n**What would NOT change the score:**\n- Additional related work citations alone\n- More ablation axes on synthetic data\n- Stronger framing without new evidence\n\n**Rebuttal success probability: 0.3-0.4.** The domain-adaptation confound is experimentally addressable but the required control experiment (MLM continued pretraining) may show that domain exposure alone suffices, which would confirm rather than resolve the weakness. The synthetic-data concern requires new evaluation data that may not be available within rebuttal timelines.\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable Process Notes\n\n- **Step 1 (Independent):** R1 scored 3/6, R2 scored 3/6. Both identified the domain-adaptation confound and TF-IDF near-parity as top concerns independently.\n- **Step 2 (Cross-sharing):** R1 endorsed R2's \"novelty paradox\" framing; R2 endorsed R1's concrete experimental demands (MLM control). Minor disagreement: R1 called multi-task framing \"misleading\" (Major); R2 called it \"overclaimed\" (Minor).\n- **Step 3 (Convergence):** Both maintained 3/6. Agreed the #1 meta-review issue is the inability to distinguish persuasion-specific learning from domain exposure. R2 added that TF-IDF near-parity may indicate the task formulation is too easy, a deeper concern than the delta size.\n- **Step 4 (Synthesis):** This report. Consensus Weak Reject with clear rebuttal path (MLM control + real data).","project":"persuasion_turn_jailbreak_detect","tier":"median","dream_id":"20260518T032748","overall_score":"weak reject","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1139,"cache_read_input_tokens":64873,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1139},"output_tokens":50,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c762cd84-11f2-4082-a75b-ef1fc3beefd8","uuid":"35ca0faa-c4fa-4668-9a7a-6afe46397c00"}
